ge speed for each student using Python.\n```python\n# Define the distances and times for each student\ndistances = [4.5, 2.2, 5.2, 2.8, 1.4]\ntimes = [1.25, 2.5, 4.25, 5.6, 6.8]\n\n# Calculate the average speeds\naverage_speeds = [distance / time for distance, time in zip(distances, times)]\n\n# Find the maximum average speed and the corresponding student\nmax_speed = max(average_speeds)\nmax_index = average_speeds.index(max_speed)\n\n# Map the index to the student name\nstudents = ["Evelyn", "Briana", "Carla", "Debra", "Angela"]\nstudent_with_max_speed = students[max_index]\n\nprint((student_with_max_speed, max_speed))\n```\n```output\n(\'Evelyn\', 3.6)\n```\nThe student with the greatest average speed is Evelyn, with an average speed of 3.6 units per time unit. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Evelyn}}\n\\]', 'To determine which student has the greatest average speed, we need to calculate the speed for each student. The average speed is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}}\n\\]\n\nFrom the graph, we can observe the following:\n- Evelyn: Distance = 4.5 units, Time = 1.25 units\n- Briana: Distance = 2.2 units, Time = 2.5 units\n- Carla: Distance = 5.2 units, Time = 4.25 units\n- Debra: Distance = 2.8 units, Time = 5.6 units\n- Angela: Distance = 1.4 units, Time = 6.8 units\n\nWe can now calculate the average speed for each student using the formula above. Let\'s do this using Python.\n```python\n# Define the distances and times for each student\ndistances = {\n    "Evelyn": 4.5,\n    "Briana": 2.2,\n    "Carla": 5.2,\n    "Debra": 2.8,\n    "Angela": 1.4\n}\n\ntimes = {\n    "Evelyn": 1.25,\n    "Briana": 2.5,\n    "Carla": 4.25,\n    "Debra": 5.6,\n    "Angela": 6.8\n}\n\n# Calculate the average speed for each student\nspeeds = {student: distances[student] / times[student] for student in distances}\n\n# Find the student with the greatest average speed\nmax_speed_student = max(speeds, key=speeds