um the squares: \\(36 + 81 = 117\\).\n4. Take the square root of the sum: \\(\\sqrt{117}\\).\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\sqrt{117}\\) by factoring 117 into its prime factors: \\(117 = 3^2 \\cdot 13\\). Therefore, \\(\\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{3^2 \\cdot 13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\\).\n\nSo the exact distance in simplest radical form is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]", "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we use the distance formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this case, the points are \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\). So, we can substitute \\(x_1 = 2\\), \\(y_1 = -6\\), \\(x_2 = -4\\), and \\(y_2 = 3\\) into the formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's simplify the expression inside the square root step by step:\n\n1. Calculate \\((-4) - 2\\):\n\\[\n(-4) - 2 = -6\n\\]\n2. Calculate \\(3 - (-6)\\):\n\\[\n3 - (-6) = 3 + 6 = 9\n\\]\n3. Substitute these values into the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 9^2}\n\\]\n4. Calculate the squares:\n\\[\n(-6)^2 = 36 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 9^2 = 81\n\\]\n5. Add the squares:\n\\[\n36 + 81 = 117\n\\]\n6. Take the square root:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{9 \\times 13} = \\sqrt{9} \\times \\sqrt{13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo, the distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Coordinates of the points\nx1, y1 = 2, -6\nx2, y2 = -4, 3\n\n# Calculate the distance using the distance formula\ndistance = math.sqrt((x2 - x1)**2 + (y2 - y1)**2)\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n10.816653826391969\n```\nThe calculated distance is approximately \\(10.8167\\), which matches \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\) when evaluated numerically. Therefore, the exact distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]", "To find the distance between two 