\).\n\nLet's break this down into manageable parts and implement it in Python using sympy.\n\n1. Translate \\(z\\) by subtracting \\(c\\):\n\\[ z' = z - c = (2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i) - (2 - 3i) = \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2} - 3)i = \\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\]\n\n2. Rotate \\(z'\\) by \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\):\n\\[ z'' = z' \\cdot e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{4}} = (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\cdot \\left(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} + i \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\]\n\\[ z'' = (\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\cdot \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}) = (1 + i - 3i + 3) = 4 - 2i \\]\n\n3. Translate back by adding \\(c\\):\n\\[ w = z'' + c = (4 - 2i) + (2 - 3i) = 6 - 5i \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify the result:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\nz = 2 + sp.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3*sp.sqrt(2))*sp.I\nc = 2 - 3*sp.I\n\n# Translate z by subtracting c\nz_prime = z - c\n\n# Rotate z_prime by pi/4\nrotation_factor = sp.exp(sp.I * sp.pi / 4)\nz_double_prime = z_prime * rotation_factor\n\n# Translate back by adding c\nw = z_double_prime + c\n\n# Simplify the result\nw_simplified = sp.simplify(w)\nprint(w_simplified)\n```\n```output\n6 - 5*I\n```\nThe result of rotating the complex number \\(z = 2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i\\) around the complex number \\(c = 2 - 3i\\) by an angle of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) counter-clockwise is \\(\\boxed{6 - 5i}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to perform a rotation of the complex number \\(z\\) around the complex number \\(c\\) by an angle of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) counter-clockwise. The formula for rotating a complex number \\(z\\) around another complex number \\(c\\) by an angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ w = c + (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\theta} \\]\n\nHere, \\(z = 2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i\\), \\(c = 2 - 3i\\), and \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\). The complex exponential \\(e^{i\\theta}\\) for \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) is:\n\n\\[ e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i \\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute \\(z - c\\):\n\n\\[ z - c = (2 + \\sqrt{2} - (3 + 3\\sqrt{2})i) - (2 - 3i) = \\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\]\n\nNext, we need to multiply \\(z - c\\) by \\(e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\):\n\n\\[ (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i\\right) \\]\n\nLet's compute this product:\n\n\\[ (\\sqrt{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i\\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 3\\sqrt{2}i \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i \\]\n\\[ = 1 + i - 3i + 3 = 4 - 2i \\]\n\nFinally, we add \\(c\\) to this result to get \\(w\\):\n\n\\[ w = c + (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = (2 - 3i) + (4 - 2i) = 6 - 5i \\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6 - 5i} \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\r\nz = 2 + cmath.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3 * cmath.sqrt(2)) * 1j\r\nc = 2 - 3j\r\n\r\n# Define the angle of rotation\r\ntheta = cmath.pi / 4\r\n\r\n# Compute the rotation\r\nrotation_factor = cmath.exp(1j * theta)\r\nw = c + (z - c) * rotation_factor\r\n\r\nprint(w)\n```", "To find the complex number \\( w \\) after rotating \\( z \\) around \\( c \\) by \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) counter-clockwise, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Translate the complex plane so that \\( c \\) is at the origin. This is done by subtracting \\( c \\) from \\( z \\).\n2. Rotate the translated \\( z \\) by \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) counter-clockwise. This is done by multiplying the translated \\( z \\) by \\( e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = \\frac{1+i}{\\sq