e is at \\(y = 1\\).\n3. The period of the sine wave is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{b}\\). From the graph, we can see that the period is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\), so \\(b = 3\\).\n4. The phase shift \\(c\\) can be determined by looking at the horizontal shift of the sine wave. The standard sine wave \\( \\sin(x) \\) reaches its maximum at \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). In the given graph, the function \\( y = 2 \\sin(3x + c) + 1 \\) reaches its maximum at \\(x = 0\\). This means that \\(3 \\cdot 0 + c = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi\\) for some integer \\(k\\). The smallest possible value of \\(c\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx = sp.symbols('x')\na = 2\nb = 3\nd = 1\n\n# The general form of the function is y = a*sin(b*x + c) + d\n# We need to find the phase shift c\n# The function reaches its maximum at x = 0, so we set up the equation 3*0 + c = pi/2\nc = sp.pi / 2\n\n# The smallest possible value of c\nprint(c)\n```\n```output\npi/2\n```\nThe smallest possible value of \\(c\\) for the function \\(y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d\\) given the graph is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\).", "To find the smallest possible value of \\(c\\) in the function \\(y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d\\), we need to analyze the given graph and match it to the standard form of the sine function.\n\nThe function \\(y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d\\) has the following properties:\n- \\(a\\) is the amplitude.\n- \\(b\\) affects the period of the sine wave.\n- \\(c\\) is the phase shift.\n- \\(d\\) is the vertical shift.\n\nFrom the graph, we can observe the following:\n1. The amplitude \\(a\\) is 2 because the maximum value of \\(y\\) is 3 and the minimum value of \\(y\\) is -1, and the amplitude is half the distance between the maximum and minimum values.\n2. The period of the sine wave is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{b}\\). From the graph, we can see that the period is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\), so \\(b = 3\\).\n3. The vertical shift \\(d\\) is 1 