 calculate the compound interest rate that would allow the worker to have at least $66,200 in his savings account by the end of the third year, given that he deposits $20,000 at the end of each year.\n\nLet's denote the annual interest rate by \\( r \\). The worker makes three deposits of $20,000 at the end of each year. The first deposit will compound for 2 years, the second deposit will compound for 1 year, and the third deposit will not compound at all. Therefore, the total amount in the account at the end of the third year can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r)^2 + 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r) + 20,000 = 66,200 \\]\n\nWe can simplify this equation to solve for \\( r \\):\n\n\\[ 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r)^2 + 20,000 \\cdot (1 + r) + 20,000 = 66,200 \\]\n\\[ 20,000 \\cdot [(1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1] = 66,200 \\]\n\\[ (1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1 = \\frac{66,200}{20,000} \\]\n\\[ (1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1 = 3.31 \\]\n\\[ (1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) - 2.31 = 0 \\]\n\nLet's denote \\( x = 1 + r \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ x^2 + x - 2.31 = 0 \\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = -2.31 \\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-2.31)}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 9.24}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{10.24}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1 \\pm 3.2}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{2.2}{2} = 1.1 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-4.2}{2} = -2.1 \\]\n\nSince \\( x = 1 + r \\) and \\( r \\) must be a positive interest rate, we take the positive solution:\n\n\\[ x = 1.1 \\]\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.1 \\]\n\\[ r = 0.1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimal compound interest rate is \\( 10\\% \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nr = symbols('r')\r\n\r\n# Define the equation\r\nequation = Eq(