( \\frac{5 + x}{2}, \\frac{3 + y}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n\\]\nThis gives us two equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6\n\\]\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[\n5 + x = 13 \\implies x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n3 + y = 12 \\implies y = 9\n\\]\nSo the coordinates of the fourth vertex are (8, 9). Therefore, the value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(8 + 9 = 17\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given coordinates of three vertices of the parallelogram\nx1, y1 = 5, 3\nx2, y2 = 6, 8\nx3, y3 = 7, 4\n\n# Midpoint of the diagonal connecting (6, 8) and (7, 4)\nmid_x = (x2 + x3) / 2\nmid_y = (y2 + y3) / 2\n\n# Solving for the fourth vertex (x, y)\nx = 2 * mid_x - x1\ny = 2 * mid_y - y1\n\n# Calculating x + y\nresult = x + y\nprint((x, y, result))\n```\n```output\n(8.0, 9.0, 17.0)\n```\nThe coordinates of the fourth vertex are \\((8, 9)\\), and the value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(8 + 9 = 17\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to use the properties of a parallelogram. In a parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other, meaning that the midpoint of one diagonal is equal to the midpoint of the other diagonal.\n\nGiven the coordinates of three vertices of the parallelogram as (5, 3), (6, 8), and (7, 4), let's denote the fourth vertex as $(x, y)$. The midpoint of the diagonal connecting (5, 3) and $(x, y)$ should be the same as the midpoint of the diagonal connecting (6, 8) and (7, 4).\n\nThe midpoint of the diagonal connecting (5, 3) and $(x, y)$ is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{5 + x}{2}, \\frac{3 + y}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of the diagonal connecting (6, 8) and (7, 4) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + 7}{2}, \\frac{8 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n\\]\n\nSince these midpoints are the same, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations will give 