on\nquotient, remainder = sp.div(dividend, divisor)\n\nprint((quotient, remainder))\n```\n```output\n(x**5 - x**4 + x**3 - x**2 + x - 1, -2)\n```\nThe quotient when \\(x^6 - 3\\) is divided by \\(x + 1\\) is \\(x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1\\) and the remainder is \\(-2\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1}\n\\]", "To find the quotient when \\(x^6 - 3\\) is divided by \\(x + 1\\), we can use polynomial long division. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Set up the division problem.\n2. Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor to get the first term of the quotient.\n3. Multiply the entire divisor by this term and subtract the result from the dividend.\n4. Repeat the process with the resulting polynomial until the degree of the remainder is less than the degree of the divisor.\n\nLet's perform the polynomial long division step-by-step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the dividend and divisor\ndividend = x**6 - 3\ndivisor = x + 1\n\n# Perform the polynomial division\nquotient, remainder = sp.div(dividend, divisor, domain='QQ')\n\n# Print the quotient\nprint(quotient)\n```\n```output\nx**5 - x**4 + x**3 - x**2 + x - 1\n```\nThe quotient when \\(x^6 - 3\\) is divided by \\(x + 1\\) is \\(x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1}\\).", "To find the quotient when \\(x^6 - 3\\) is divided by \\(x + 1\\), we can use polynomial long division. Here's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Set up the division problem with \\(x^6 - 3\\) as the dividend and \\(x + 1\\) as the divisor.\n2. Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor to get the first term of the quotient.\n3. Multiply the entire divisor by this term and subtract the result from the dividend.\n4. Repeat the process with the resulting polynomial until the degree of the remainder is 