of students participating in the science club, \\( |B| = 21 \\)\n- Number of students participating in neither, \\( |U - (A \\cup B)| = 6 \\)\n\nFrom the above, we can find \\( |A \\cup B| \\) as follows:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |U| - \\text{number of students participating in neither} = 50 - 6 = 44 \\]\n\nNow we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\( \\boxed{5} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nmathcounts_students = 28\nscience_club_students = 21\nneither_students = 6\n\n# Calculate the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\neither_or_both_students = total_students - neither_students\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\nboth_students = mathcounts_students + science_club_students - either_or_both_students\nprint(both_students)\n```", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following:\n\n- \\( |A| \\) = number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS = 28\n- \\( |B| \\) = number of students participating in science club = 21\n- \\( |A \\cup B| \\) = number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both\n- \\( |A \\cap B| \\) = number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and science club (this is what we need to find)\n- \\( N \\) = total number of students = 50\n- \\( N - |A \\cup B| \\) = number of students participating in neither MATHCOUNTS nor science club = 6\n\nFrom the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we know that:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nWe also know that:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = N - \\text{number of students participating in neither} = 50 - 6 = 44 \\]\n\nSo we can substitute the known values into the inclusion-exclusion formula:\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nSolving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nmathcounts_students = 28\nscience_club_students = 21\nneither_students = 6\n\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both\neither_or_both_students = total_students - neither_students\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\nboth_students = mathcounts_students + science_club_students - either_or_both_students\nprint(both_students)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following:\n\n- \\( A \\) as the set of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\( B \\) as the set of students participating in the science club.\n- \\( |A| \\) as the number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS, which is 28.\n- \\( |B| \\) as the number of students participating in the science club, which is 21.\n- \\( |A \\cup B| \\) as the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both. Since 6 students do not participate in either, the number of students participating in at least one of the activities is \\( 50 - 6 = 44 \\).\n- \\( |A \\cap B| \\) as the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club, which is what we need to find.\n\nAccording to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nSolving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both 