\r\n# Compute the singular values of A\r\nsingular_values = np.linalg.svd(A, compute_uv=False)\r\n\r\n# The largest singular value is the maximum of the singular values\r\nC = np.max(singular_values)\r\nprint(C)\n```\n```output\n3.9999999999999996\n```\nThe computed largest singular value of the matrix \\( A \\) is approximately 4, confirming our analytical solution. Therefore, the smallest positive real number \\( C \\) for which\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{v} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\n\\]\nfor all two-dimensional vectors \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest positive real number \\( C \\) such that for any two-dimensional vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), the inequality \\(\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{v} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\\) holds.\n\nFirst, let's denote the matrix as \\( A = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\). For any vector \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\), we have:\n\\[ A \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x + 3y \\\\ -2y \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nThe norm of \\( A \\mathbf{v} \\) is:\n\\[ \\left\\| A \\mathbf{v} \\right\\| = \\sqrt{(2x + 3y)^2 + (-2y)^2} = \\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 9y^2 + 4y^2} = \\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2}. \\]\nThe norm of \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is:\n\\[ \\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}. \\]\nWe need to find the maximum value of the ratio \\(\\frac{\\left\\| A \\mathbf{v} \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}\\) for all non-zero vectors \\(\\mathbf{v}\\). This ratio can be written as:\n\\[ \\frac{\\left\\| A \\mathbf{v} \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|} = \\frac{\\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2}}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2}{x^2 + y^2}}. \\]\nTo find the maximum value of this expression, we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or consider the eigenvalues of the matrix \\( A^T A \\). The maximum value of the ratio is the square root of the maximum eigenvalue of \\( A^T A \\).\n\nFirst, let's compute \\( A^T A \\):\n\\[ A^T = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\]\n\\[ A^T A = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 13 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nThe characteristic polynomial of \\( A^T A \\) is given by:\n\\[ \\det(A^T A - \\lambda I) = \\begin{vmatrix} 4 - \\lambda & 6 \\\\ 6 & 13 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix} = (4 - \\lambda)(13 - \\lambda) - 36 = \\lambda^2 - 17\\lambda + 16. \\]\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - 17\\lambda + 16 = 0. \\]\nSolving this quadratic equation, we get:\n\\[ \\lambda = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{17^2 - 4 \\cdot 16}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{289 - 64}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{225}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm 15}{2}. \\]\nSo, the eigenvalues are \\( \\lambda_1 = 16 \\) and \\( \\lambda_2 = 1 \\). The maximum eigenvalue is \\( \\lambda_{\\text{max}} = 16 \\). Therefore, the maximum value of the ratio \\(\\frac{\\left\\| A \\mathbf{v} \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}\\) is \\(\\sqrt{16} = 4\\).\n\nThus, the smallest positive real number \\( C \\) for which the inequality \\(\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{v} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\\) holds for all two-dimensional vectors \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is \\( C = 4 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]", "To find the smallest positive real number \\( C \\) for which the inequality\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{v} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\n\\]\nholds for all two-dimensional vectors \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), we need to understand how the matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) transforms vectors and how its norm behaves.\n\nThe norm of a matrix \\( A \\) is defined as the maximum of the norms of the vectors \\( A \\mathbf{v} \\) divided by the norms of the vectors \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), i.e.,\n\\[\n\\| A \\| = \\max_{\\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}} \\frac{\\| A \\mathbf{v} \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{v} \\|}.\n\\]\nThis is equivalent to the largest singular value of the matrix \\( A \\).\n\nThe singular values of a matrix \\( A \\) are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix \\( A^T A \\). Let's compute the singular values of the matrix \\( A = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nFirst, we compute \\( A^T A \\):\n\\[\nA^T A = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 13 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of \\( A^T A \\). The characteristic polynomial of \\( A^T A \\) is given by\n\\[\n\\det \\left( \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 13 \\end{pmatrix} - \\lambda \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\right) = \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - \\lambda & 6 \\\\ 6 & 13 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (4 - \\lambda)(13 - \\lambda) - 36 = \\lambda^2 - 17\\lambda + 16.\n\\]\nThe roots of the characteristic polynomial \\(\\lambda^2 - 17\\lambda + 16 = 0\\) are\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{17^2 - 4 \\cdot 16}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{289 - 64}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{225}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm 15}{2}.\n\\]\nSo, the eigenvalues are \\( \\lambda_1 = 16 \\) and \\( \\lambda_2 = 1 \\). The singular values of \\( A \\) are the square roots of these eigenvalues, which are \\( \\sqrt{16} = 4 \\) and \\( \\sqrt{1} = 1 \\). The largest singular value is \\( 4 \\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive real number \\( C \\) for which the inequality holds is \\( 4 \\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the matrix A\nA = np.array([[2, 3], [0, -2]])\n\n# Compute the singular values of A\nsingular_values = np.linalg.svd(A, compute_uv=False)\n\n# The largest singular value is the norm of the matrix A\nC = np.max(singular_values)\nprint(C)\n```\n```output\n3.6055