 minimum \\(y\\)-coordinate of \\(A\\)). So, \\(y_2 - y_1 = 3 - 0 = 3\\).\n\nThe minimum value of \\(x_2 - x_1\\) occurs when \\(x_2 = 4\\) (the minimum \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(B\\)) and \\(x_1 = 2\\) (the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(A\\)). So, \\(x_2 - x_1 = 4 - 2 = 2\\).\n\nThus, the maximum slope is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the ranges for the coordinates of points A and B\nx1_range = range(0, 3)  # x1 can be 0, 1, or 2\ny1_range = range(0, 3)  # y1 can be 0, 1, or 2\n\nx2_range = range(4, 6)  # x2 can be 4 or 5\ny2_range = range(2, 4)  # y2 can be 2 or 3\n\n# Initialize the maximum slope\nmax_slope = 0\n\n# Iterate over all possible combinations of (x1, y1) and (x2, y2)\nfor x1 in x1_range:\n    for y1 in y1_range:\n        for x2 in x2_range:\n            for y2 in y2_range:\n                # Calculate the slope\n                slope = (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1)\n                # Update the maximum slope if the current slope is greater\n                if slope > max_slope:\n                    max_slope = slope\n\nprint(max_slope)\n```\n```output\n1.5\n```\nThe greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\n\\]", "To find the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we need to consider the coordinates of the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) within or on the squares defined. The slope \\(m\\) of a line passing through two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\]\n\nPoint \\(A\\) can have coordinates \\((x_1, y_1)\\) where \\(0 \\leq x_1 \\leq 2\\) and \\(0 \\leq y_1 \\leq 2\\). Point \\(B\\) can have coordinates \\((x_2, y_2)\\) where \\(4 \\leq x_2 \\leq 5\\) and \\(2 \\leq y_2 \\leq 3\\).\n\nTo maximize the slope, we need to maximize the numerator \\(y_2 -