 points that give the maximum difference in \\(y\\)-coordinates divided by the minimum difference in \\(x\\)-coordinates. The maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate for \\(A\\) is 2, and the minimum \\(y\\)-coordinate for \\(B\\) is 2, so the maximum difference in \\(y\\)-coordinates is \\(3 - 0 = 3\\). The minimum \\(x\\)-coordinate for \\(A\\) is 0, and the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate for \\(B\\) is 5, so the minimum difference in \\(x\\)-coordinates is \\(4 - 0 = 4\\).\n\nThus, the maximum possible slope is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 0}{4 - 0} = \\frac{3}{4} \\]\n\nWe can confirm this by considering the coordinates of the corners of the squares. The point \\(A\\) with the minimum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\((0, 2)\\), and the point \\(B\\) with the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate and minimum \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\((5, 2)\\). The slope of the line containing these points is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{2 - 2}{5 - 0} = \\frac{0}{5} = 0 \\]\n\nHowever, if we consider the point \\(A\\) with the minimum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate \\((0, 2)\\), and the point \\(B\\) with the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate \\((5, 3)\\), the slope of the line containing these points is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 2}{5 - 0} = \\frac{1}{5} \\]\n\nThe greatest possible value of the slope is achieved by considering the point \\(A\\) with the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate \\((2, 2)\\), and the point \\(B\\) with the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate \\((5, 3)\\). The slope of the line containing these points is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 2}{5 - 2} = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nThe greatest possible value of the slope is achieved by considering the point \\(A\\) with the minimum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate \\((0, 2)\\), and the point \\(B\\) with the maximum \\(x\\)-coordinate and maximum \\(y\\)-coordinate \\((5, 3)\\). The slope of the line containing these points is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3 - 2}{5 - 0