k which of these solutions satisfy the original equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\).\n\n1. For \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[|5(1) - 1| = 1 + 3\\]\n\\[|5 - 1| = 4\\]\n\\[|4| = 4\\]\n\\[4 = 4\\]\nThis is true, so \\(x = 1\\) is a valid solution.\n\n2. For \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\\[|5\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 1| = -\\frac{1}{3} + 3\\]\n\\[|-\\frac{5}{3} - 1| = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\n\\[|-\\frac{5}{3} - \\frac{3}{3}| = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\n\\[|-\\frac{8}{3}| = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\n\\[\\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\nThis is true, so \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\) is also a valid solution.\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation, the answer is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Solutions found\r\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# Check which solutions are valid\r\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in solutions if check_solution(x)]\r\n\r\n# Find the largest valid solution\r\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\r\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the equation \\(|5x-1|=x+3\\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The expression \\(|5x-1|\\) can be split into two cases:\n\n1. \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n2. \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n### Case 1: \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n\\[5x - 1 = x + 3\\]\n\\[5x - x = 3 + 1\\]\n\\[4x = 4\\]\n\\[x = 1\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\\[5x - 1 = -x - 3\\]\n\\[5x + x = -3 + 1\\]\n\\[6x = -2\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\]\n\nSo, the potential solutions are \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\). We need to verify that these solutions satisfy the original equation \\(|5x-1|=x+3\\).\n\n### Verification\n\n1. For \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[|5(1) - 1| = 1 + 3\\]\n\\[|5 - 1| = 4\\]\n\\[4 = 4\\]\nThis is true,