ases}\nax^2 & \\text{if } x \\geq a,\\\\\nax + 2a & \\text{if } x < a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\geq a\\), the function is \\(f(x) = ax^2\\), which is a parabola opening upwards. For \\(x < a\\), the function is \\(f(x) = ax + 2a\\), which is a line with slope \\(a\\).\n\nTo ensure that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once, the function must cover all possible \\(y\\)-values. This means that the minimum value of the parabola \\(ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\) must be less than or equal to the maximum value of the line \\(ax + 2a\\) for \\(x < a\\).\n\n1. The minimum value of \\(ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\) is \\(a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\), which occurs at \\(x = a\\).\n2. The maximum value of \\(ax + 2a\\) for \\(x < a\\) approaches \\(a \\cdot a + 2a = a^2 + 2a\\) as \\(x\\) approaches \\(a\\) from the left.\n\nFor the function to intersect every horizontal line at least once, the minimum value of the parabola must be less than or equal to the maximum value of the line:\n\\[a^3 \\leq a^2 + 2a.\\]\n\nWe can solve this inequality to find the largest value of \\(a\\):\n\\[a^3 - a^2 - 2a \\leq 0.\\]\n\\[a(a^2 - a - 2) \\leq 0.\\]\n\\[a(a - 2)(a + 1) \\leq 0.\\]\n\nThe roots of the equation \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1) = 0\\) are \\(a = 0\\), \\(a = 2\\), and \\(a = -1\\). We need to check the intervals determined by these roots to find where the inequality holds true.\n\n- For \\(a < -1\\), the expression \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1)\\) is positive.\n- For \\(-1 \\leq a \\leq 0\\), the expression \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1)\\) is non-positive.\n- For \\(0 < a < 2\\), the expression \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1)\\) is negative.\n- For \\(a \\geq 2\\), the expression \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1)\\) is non-negative.\n\nThe largest value of \\(a\\) that satisfies the inequality is \\(a = 2\\). Therefore, the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once is \\(a = 2\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]', 'To dete