ese steps:\n\n1. Calculate the value of \\(a\\).\n2. Find the coefficient of the \\(x^3\\) term in the expansion of \\(\\left(ax + \\frac{1}{2ax}\\right)^9\\).\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the value of \\(a\\)\nThe integral \\(a = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} (-\\cos x) \\, dx\\) can be evaluated as follows:\n\\[\na = -\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cos x \\, dx = -\\left[\\sin x\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} = -\\left(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\sin 0\\right) = -1.\n\\]\nSo, \\(a = -1\\).\n\n### Step 2: Find the coefficient of the \\(x^3\\) term in the expansion of \\(\\left(ax + \\frac{1}{2ax}\\right)^9\\)\nWe need to find the coefficient of the \\(x^3\\) term in the expansion of \\(\\left(-x + \\frac{1}{2(-x)}\\right)^9 = \\left(-x - \\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^9 = \\left(-\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2x}\\right)\\right)^9 = (-1)^9 \\left(x + \\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^9 = -\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^9\\).\n\nWe use the binomial theorem to expand \\(\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^9\\). The general term in the expansion is given by:\n\\[\n\\binom{9}{k} x^{9-k} \\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^k = \\binom{9}{k} x^{9-k} \\frac{1}{2^k x^k} = \\binom{9}{k} \\frac{1}{2^k} x^{9-2k}.\n\\]\nWe need the term where the exponent of \\(x\\) is 3, so we set \\(9 - 2k = 3\\). Solving for \\(k\\), we get \\(k = 3\\). The coefficient of the \\(x^3\\) term is then:\n\\[\n-\\binom{9}{3} \\frac{1}{2^3} = -\\frac{9!}{3!6!} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} = -\\frac{9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7}{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} = -\\frac{84}{8} = -\\frac{21}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSo, the coefficient of the \\(x^3\\) term is \\(-\\frac{21}{2}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Calculate the value of a\na = sp.integrate(-sp.cos(x), (x, 0, sp.pi/2))\n\n# Define the expression\nexpr = (a*x + 1/(2*a*x))**9\n\n# Expand the expression\nexpanded_expr = sp.expand(expr)\n\n# Extract the coefficient of x^3\ncoeff_x3 = sp.collect(expanded_expr, 