) \\implies x = \\frac{\\omega}{1 + 13\\omega}.\\]\n\n2. **Identify the Roots:**\n   The roots are of the form \\(r_k = \\frac{\\omega_k}{1 + 13\\omega_k}\\), where \\(\\omega_k\\) are the 10th roots of \\(-1\\). The 10th roots of \\(-1\\) are given by:\n   \\[\\omega_k = e^{i\\pi (2k+1)/10}, \\quad k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9.\\]\n\n3. **Find the Product of Conjugate Pairs:**\n   For each root \\(r_k = \\frac{\\omega_k}{1 + 13\\omega_k}\\), the conjugate \\(\\overline{r_k} = \\frac{\\overline{\\omega_k}}{1 + 13\\overline{\\omega_k}}\\). The product \\(r_k \\overline{r_k}\\) is:\n   \\[r_k \\overline{r_k} = \\left(\\frac{\\omega_k}{1 + 13\\omega_k}\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\overline{\\omega_k}}{1 + 13\\overline{\\omega_k}}\\right) = \\frac{|\\omega_k|^2}{(1 + 13\\omega_k)(1 + 13\\overline{\\omega_k})} = \\frac{1}{1 + 13(\\omega_k + \\overline{\\omega_k}) + 169} = \\frac{1}{169 + 13 \\cdot 2 \\cos\\left(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{10}\\right) + 1}.\\]\n\n4. **Sum the Reciprocals:**\n   We need to find the sum:\n   \\[\\sum_{k=0}^{4} \\frac{1}{r_k \\overline{r_k}} = \\sum_{k=0}^{4} \\left(169 + 26 \\cos\\left(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{10}\\right) + 1\\right) = 5 \\cdot 170 + 26 \\sum_{k=0}^{4} \\cos\\left(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{10}\\right).\\]\n   The sum of cosines of the 10th roots of \\(-1\\) is zero, so the final answer is:\n   \\[5 \\cdot 170 = 850.\\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to verify our reasoning.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the 10th roots of -1\nomega = sp.exp(sp.I * sp.pi / 10)\nroots = [omega**(2*k + 1) for k in range(10)]\n\n# Calculate the product of conjugate pairs\nsum_reciprocals = 0\nfor root in roots:\n    r = root / (1 + 13 * root)\n    r_conj = sp.conjugate(r)\n    product = r * r_conj\n    sum_reciprocals += 1 / product\n\n# Simplify the result\nsum_reciprocals = sp.simplify(sum_reciprocals)\nprint(sum_reciprocals)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that the code execution too