 formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27 \\pm 4\\sqrt{41}\\).\n\nFor \\(x^2 + 6x - 27 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 + 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x^2 + 6x - 27 + 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 - 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 - 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the points of intersection are \\((-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\), \\((-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\), \\((-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\), and \\((-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\).\n\nNext, we need to compute the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\). The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is \\(\\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\\).\n\nLet's calculate the distances:\n\n1. Distance from \\((-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\) to \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}})^2 + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{4(9 + \\sqrt{41}) + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{36 + 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{141 + 20\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\n2. Distance from \\((-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\) to \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}})^2 + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{4(9 + \\sqrt{41}) + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{36 + 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{141 + 20\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\n3. Distance from \\((-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\) to \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}})^2 + (8 - \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{4(9 - \\sqr