he terms of the sequence can be expressed in terms of $a$ and some common ratios and differences.\n\n1. For the geometric subsequence $a_{2k-1}$, $a_{2k}$, $a_{2k+1}$, we have:\n   \\[\n   a_{2k}^2 = a_{2k-1} \\cdot a_{2k+1}\n   \\]\n2. For the arithmetic subsequence $a_{2k}$, $a_{2k+1}$, $a_{2k+2}$, we have:\n   \\[\n   2a_{2k+1} = a_{2k} + a_{2k+2}\n   \\]\n\nWe can start by expressing the terms in the sequence using these properties. Let's denote the common ratio of the geometric subsequence by $r_k$ and the common difference of the arithmetic subsequence by $d_k$.\n\nFor $k = 1$, we have:\n\\[\na_1 = a, \\quad a_2 = ar_1, \\quad a_3 = ar_1^2\n\\]\nFor $k = 2$, we have:\n\\[\na_3 = ar_1^2, \\quad a_4 = ar_1^2 + d_2, \\quad a_5 = ar_1^2 + 2d_2\n\\]\nFor $k = 3$, we have:\n\\[\na_5 = ar_1^2 + 2d_2, \\quad a_6 = (ar_1^2 + 2d_2)r_3, \\quad a_7 = (ar_1^2 + 2d_2)r_3^2\n\\]\nFor $k = 4$, we have:\n\\[\na_7 = (ar_1^2 + 2d_2)r_3^2, \\quad a_8 = (ar_1^2 + 2d_2)r_3^2 + d_4, \\quad a_9 = (ar_1^2 + 2d_2)r_3^2 + 2d_4\n\\]\nAnd so on.\n\nWe can see that the terms of the sequence are related in a specific way. To find the value of $a_1$, we need to use the given information that $a_{13} = 2016$. We can express $a_{13}$ in terms of $a$ and the common ratios and differences.\n\nLet's denote the common ratio for the geometric subsequence by $r$ and the common difference for the arithmetic subsequence by $d$. We can express the terms of the sequence as follows:\n\\[\na_1 = a, \\quad a_2 = ar, \\quad a_3 = ar^2\n\\]\n\\[\na_4 = ar^2 + d, \\quad a_5 = ar^2 + 2d\n\\]\n\\[\na_6 = (ar^2 + 2d)r, \\quad a_7 = (ar^2 + 2d)r^2\n\\]\n\\[\na_8 = (ar^2 + 2d)r^2 + d, \\quad a_9 = (ar^2 + 2d)r^2 + 2d\n\\]\n\\[\na_{10} = ((ar^2 + 2d)r^2 + 2d)r, \\quad a_{11} = ((ar^2 + 2d)r^2 + 2d)r^2\n\\]\n\\[\na_{12} = ((ar^2 + 2d)r^2 + 2d)r^2 + d, \\quad a_{13} = ((ar^2 + 2d)r^2 + 2d)r^2 + 2d\n\\]\n\nWe know that $a_{13} = 2016$. Let's substitute the expressions for the terms of the sequence into this equation a