he count of valid pairs\ncount = 0\n\n# Iterate over all possible integer values for a and b in the range [-5, 5]\nfor a in range(-5, 6):\n    for b in range(-5, 6):\n        if a**2 + b**2 <= 25:\n            count += 1\n\n# Print the final count of valid pairs\nprint(count)\n```\n```output\n81\n```\nThe number of ordered pairs \\((a, b)\\) of integers such that \\(|a + bi| \\le 5\\) is \\(\\boxed{81}\\).", 'To solve this problem, we need to explore all possible ways of adding parentheses to the expression \\(1 + 2 + 3 - 4 + 5 + 6\\) and calculate the resulting values. We can use Python to systematically evaluate each possible placement of parentheses and find the minimum value.\n\nHere\'s the plan:\n1. Generate all possible ways to add parentheses to the expression.\n2. Evaluate each expression to find its value.\n3. Determine the minimum value from these evaluations.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python:\n\n```python\nfrom itertools import product\n\n# The original expression without parentheses\nexpression = "1 + 2 + 3 - 4 + 5 + 6"\n\n# Possible ways to add parentheses\n# We can add parentheses around any two consecutive numbers or around the entire expression\n# For simplicity, we will consider all possible ways to split the expression into two parts\n# and evaluate each part separately.\n\n# Generate all possible splits of the expression\nsplits = []\nfor i in range(1, len(expression)):\n    left = expression[:i]\n    right = expression[i:]\n    splits.append((left, right))\n\n# Evaluate each possible split\nmin_value = float(\'inf\')\nfor left, right in splits:\n    # Evaluate the left and right parts separately\n    left_value = eval(left)\n    right_value = eval(right)\n    # Combine the results\n    value = left_value + right_value\n    # Update the minimum value\n    if value < min_value:\n        min_value = value\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name \'inf\' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the use of `float(\'inf\')`. 