6| = 3x-6 \\)\n   - Equation: \\( x+5 - (3x-6) = 0 \\)\n   - Simplified: \\( -2x+11 = 0 \\)\n   - Solution: \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\) (in the interval)\n\nThe solutions are \\( x = \\frac{1}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\). The largest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python by checking the solutions in the different intervals.\n```python\n# Define the intervals and check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x_val):\r\n    if x_val < -5:\r\n        return -(x_val + 5) - (-(3 * x_val - 6)) == 0\r\n    elif -5 <= x_val < 2:\r\n        return (x_val + 5) - (-(3 * x_val - 6)) == 0\r\n    else:\r\n        return (x_val + 5) - (3 * x_val - 6) == 0\r\n\r\n# Check the solutions\r\nsolutions = [1/4, 11/2]\r\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in solutions if check_solution(x)]\r\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\r\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n5.5\n```\nThe largest possible value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation \\( |x+5| - |3x-6| = 0 \\) is \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\), which is equivalent to 5.5.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{11}{2}}\n\\]", 'To solve the equation \\(|x+5| - |3x-6| = 0\\), we need to consider the different cases for the absolute value expressions. The critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign are \\(x = -5\\) and \\(x = 2\\). These points divide the number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, -5)\\), \\([-5, 2]\\), and \\((2, \\infty)\\). We will solve the equation in each of these intervals.\n\n1. For \\(x < -5\\):\n   \\[\n   |x+5| = -(x+5) = -x-5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad |3x-6| = -(3x-6) = -3x+6\n   \\]\n   So the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   -x-5 - (-3x+6) = 0 \\implies -x-5 + 3x-6 = 0 \\implies 2x-11 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{11}{2}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\frac{11}{2}\\) is not in the interval \\((-\\infty, -5)\\), we discard this solution.\n\n2. For \\(-5 \\leq x \\leq 2\\):\n   \\[\n   |x+5| = x+5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad |3x-6