( |3x-6| = 3x-6 \\).\n   - The equation becomes \\( x+5 - (3x-6) = 0 \\), which simplifies to \\( x+5 - 3x + 6 = 0 \\) or \\( -2x + 11 = 0 \\). Solving for \\( x \\), we get \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\). Since \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\) is in the interval \\( [2, \\infty) \\), we keep this solution.\n\nThe possible solutions are \\( x = \\frac{1}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\). The largest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve, Abs\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equation\nequation = Eq(Abs(x + 5) - Abs(3*x - 6), 0)\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolutions = solve(equation, x)\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\nNotImplementedError: solving Abs(3*x - 6) when the argument is not real or imaginary.\n```\nIt appears that the direct symbolic solution for absolute value equations is not implemented in the current environment. However, we can handle the absolute value equation by considering the different cases manually, as we did earlier. We will solve the equation in each of the intervals separately.\n\nLet's implement the solution in Python by considering the different cases for the absolute value expressions.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Case 1: x < -5\neq1 = Eq(-(x + 5) - (-(3*x - 6)), 0)\nsolution1 = solve(eq1, x)\n\n# Case 2: -5 <= x < 2\neq2 = Eq(x + 5 - (-(3*x - 6)), 0)\nsolution2 = solve(eq2, x)\n\n# Case 3: x >= 2\neq3 = Eq(x + 5 - (3*x - 6), 0)\nsolution3 = solve(eq3, x)\n\n# Combine all solutions and filter out the valid ones\nsolutions = [sol for sol in solution1 + solution2 + solution3 if (sol < -5 or -5 <= sol < 2 or sol >= 2)]\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[1/4, 11/2]\n```\nThe solutions to the equation \\( |x+5| - |3x-6| = 0 \\) are \\( x = \\frac{1}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\). The largest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\).\n\nThere