the coordinates of the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\):\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} \\right|. \\]\n\nSince the area is 6, we have:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} \\right| = 6. \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ \\left| \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} \\right| = 12. \\]\n\nFinally, we need to find the square of this determinant:\n\n\\[ \\left( \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} \\right)^2 = 12^2 = 144. \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3 = sp.symbols('x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = (x1 - x2)**2 + (y1 - y2)**2 - 9\r\neq2 = (x1 - x3)**2 + (y1 - y3)**2 - 16\r\neq3 = (x2 - x3)**2 + (y2 - y3)**2 - 25\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations for the coordinates\r\nsolutions = sp.solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], (x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3), dict=True)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the determinant for one of the solutions\r\ndet = sp.Matrix([[sol[x1], sol[y1], 1], [sol[x2], sol[y2], 1], [sol[x3], sol[y3], 1]]).det()\r\ndet_squared = det**2\r\n\r\nprint(det_squared)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that solving the system of equations symbolically is computationally intensive and may not be feasible within a reasonable time frame. Instead, we can use the geometric interpretation to confirm our result.\n\nGiven that the distances form a right triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5, we can place the points in a coordinate system to simplify the calculation. For example, we can place the points as follows:\n\n1. \\((x_1, y_1) = (0, 0)\\)\n2. \\((x_2, y_2) = (3, 0)\\)\n3. \\((x_3, y_3) = (0, 4)\\)\n\nWith these coordinates, we can directly