 even integers whose product is 288. Let's denote the smaller even integer by \\( x \\). Then the next consecutive even integer will be \\( x + 2 \\). According to the problem, the product of these two integers is 288, so we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ x(x + 2) = 288 \\]\n\nExpanding and rearranging the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 2x - 288 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation. We can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), and \\( c = -288 \\).\n\nLet's calculate the discriminant first:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-288) = 4 + 1152 = 1156 \\]\n\nNow we can find the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{1156}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-2 \\pm 34}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{32}{2} = 16 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-36}{2} = -18 \\]\n\nSince we are looking for positive even integers, we discard the negative solution. So, the smaller even integer is \\( x = 16 \\). The greater of the two integers is \\( x + 2 = 18 \\).\n\nLet's verify this by calculating the product of 16 and 18:\n\n\\[ 16 \\times 18 = 288 \\]\n\nThis confirms that our solution is correct. The greater of the two integers is \\( \\boxed{18} \\).", "Let the two consecutive even integers be \\(2n\\) and \\(2n+2\\). According to the problem, the product of these two integers is 288. So we can write the equation:\n\\[\n(2n) \\cdot (2n + 2) = 288\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\n4n^2 + 4n = 288\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 4, we get:\n\\[\nn^2 + n = 72\n\\]\nRearranging the equation, we get a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[\nn^2 + n - 72 = 0\n\\]\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(n = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), and \\(c = -72\\):\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-72)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 2