 \\[\n   \\sqrt{3} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\sqrt{3} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{3}\\right) = \\sqrt{3} \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. Now, combine the two simplified terms:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. To combine these terms into a single fraction, find a common denominator. The common denominator of 2 and 3 is 6:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{6} + \\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{6} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{2} + 8\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. The fraction \\(\\frac{9\\sqrt{2} + 8\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\) is already in the form \\(\\frac{a\\sqrt{2} + b\\sqrt{3}}{c}\\) with \\(a = 9\\), \\(b = 8\\), and \\(c = 6\\).\n\n6. Finally, calculate \\(a + b + c\\):\n   \\[\n   a + b + c = 9 + 8 + 6 = 23\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the terms\nfrom sympy import sqrt, Rational\n\n# Simplify the terms\nterm1 = sqrt(2) + 1/sqrt(2)\nterm2 = sqrt(3) + 1/sqrt(3)\n\n# Combine the terms into a single fraction\ncombined_term = term1 + term2\ncombined_term_simplified = combined_term.simplify()\n\n# Extract the numerator and denominator\nnumerator = combined_term_simplified.as_numer_denom()[0]\ndenominator = combined_term_simplified.as_numer_denom()[1]\n\n# Extract coefficients of sqrt(2) and sqrt(3)\na = numerator.coeff(sqrt(2))\nb = numerator.coeff(sqrt(3))\nc = denominator\n\n# Calculate a + b + c\nresult = a + b + c\nprint((result, a, b, c))\n```\n```output\n(23, 9, 8, 6)\n```\nThe values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are 9, 8, and 6 respectively. Therefore, the sum \\(a + b + c\\) is:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 9 + 8 + 6 = 23\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{23}\\).", 'We start by simplifying the expression $\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{1}{\\