ging 7 units (the "block" of two boys and the 6 girls) in a row.\n\n1. First, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange the 7 units. This is simply 7! (7 factorial).\n2. Next, we need to consider the number of ways to arrange the two boys within their "block". Since there are 2 boys, there are 2! (2 factorial) ways to arrange them.\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is the number of ways to arrange the 7 units multiplied by the number of ways to arrange the two boys within their "block".\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 7 units (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys)\nways_to_arrange_units = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Number of ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block\nways_to_arrange_boys_in_block = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = ways_to_arrange_units * ways_to_arrange_boys_in_block\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row, with the condition that the 2 boys must sit next to each other, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can think of the two boys as a single "block" or "unit" since they must sit next to each other. This means we are essentially arranging 7 units (6 girls and 1 block of 2 boys) in a row.\n\n1. First, we calculate the number of ways to arrange these 7 units. This is simply 7! (7 factorial).\n2. Next, we need to consider that the two boys within their block can switch places. There are 2! (2 factorial) ways to arrange the two boys within their block.\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is \\(7! \\times 2!\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate 7! (7 factorial)\nfactorial_7 = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Calculate 2! (2 factorial)\nfactorial_2 = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = factorial_7 * factorial_2\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total 