frac{A}{F_n} + \\frac{B}{F_{n + 1} + F_n}. \\]\n\nBy solving for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we get:\n\\[ \\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n (F_{n + 1} + F_n)} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n + 1} + F_n}. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ \\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n + 2}}. \\]\n\nNow, we can write the infinite sum as a telescoping series:\n\\[ \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n + 2}} \\right). \\]\n\nThe terms in the sum will cancel out, leaving us with:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{1}{F_1} - \\frac{1}{F_3} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_2} - \\frac{1}{F_4} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_3} - \\frac{1}{F_5} \\right) + \\cdots. \\]\n\nAll the intermediate terms cancel out, leaving:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{F_1} + \\frac{1}{F_2} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} = 2. \\]\n\nSo, the value of the infinite sum is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2}. \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Let's compute the partial sum of the series to confirm the result\r\ndef fibonacci(n):\r\n    if n == 0:\r\n        return 0\r\n    elif n == 1:\r\n        return 1\r\n    else:\r\n        a, b = 0, 1\r\n        for _ in range(n - 1):\r\n            a, b = b, a + b\r\n        return b\r\n\r\n# Compute the partial sum of the series up to a large number of terms\r\npartial_sum = sum(fibonacci(n + 1) / (fibonacci(n) * fibonacci(n + 2)) for n in range(1, 1000))\r\n\r\nprint(partial_sum)\n```", "To solve the given infinite series \\(\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}}\\), where \\(F_n\\) denotes the \\(n\\)th Fibonacci number, we need to analyze the terms and look for a pattern or simplification.\n\nFirst, let's recall the definition of the Fibonacci sequence: \\(F_0 = 0\\), \\(F_1 = 1\\), and for \\(n \\geq 2\\), \\(F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2}\\).\n\nWe start by examining the term \\(\\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}}\\). Using the Fibonacci recurrence relation, we know that \\(F_{n + 2} = F_{n + 1} + F_n\\). So, we can rewrite the term as:\n\\[\n\\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}} = \\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n (F_{n + 1} + F_n)}.\n\\]\n\nWe can use partial fraction decomposition to simplify this expression. Notice that:\n\\[\n\\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n (F_{n + 1} + F_n)} = \\frac{A}{F_n} + \\frac{B}{F_{n + 1} + F_n}.\n\\]\nBy solving for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we find that:\n\\[\n\\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n (F_{n + 1} + F_n)} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n + 1} + F_n}.\n\\]\nSince \\(F_{n + 2} = F_{n + 1} + F_n\\), we can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n\\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n + 2}}.\n\\]\n\nThis means that the series \\(\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}}\\) can be written as a telescoping series:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n + 2}} \\right).\n\\]\n\nIn a telescoping series, most terms cancel out. Specifically, we have:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{F_1} - \\frac{1}{F_3} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_2} - \\frac{1}{F_4} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_3} - \\frac{1}{F_5} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_4} - \\frac{1}{F_6} \\right) + \\cdots\n\\]\nThe terms \\(\\frac{1}{F_3}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{F_4}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{F_5}\\), etc., cancel out, leaving us with:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{F_1} + \\frac{1}{F_2} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} = 2.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Function to generate Fibonacci numbers\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    fib = [0, 1]\n    for i in range(2, n+1):\n        fib.append(fib[-1] + fib[-2])\n    return fib\n\n# Function to compute the sum of the series up to a large number of terms\ndef sum_series(terms):\n    fib = fibonacci(terms + 2)  # Generate enough Fibonacci numbers\n    total_sum = sum(fib[n + 1] / (fib[n] * fib[n + 2]) for n in range(1, terms + 1))\n    return total_sum\n\n# Compute the sum of the series for a large number of terms to approximate the infinite sum\nterms = 1000\nresult = sum_series(terms)\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the given problem, we need to find the sum of the infinite series \\(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n F_{n+2}}\\), where \\(F_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th Fibonacci number.\n\nLet's start by examining the general term of the series. Recall that the Fibonacci sequence is defined as follows:\n\\[F_0 = 0, \\quad F_1 = 1, \\quad F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n \\text{ for } n \\geq 0.\\]\n\nThe general term of the series is \\(\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n F_{n+2}}\\). Using the Fibonacci recurrence relation, we can rewrite \\(F_{n+2}\\) as \\(F_{n+1} + F_n\\). Therefore, the general term becomes:\n\\[\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n (F_{n+1} + F_n)}.\\]\n\nWe can simplify this expression by splitting it into partial fractions:\n\\[\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n (F_{n+1} + F_n)} = \\frac{A}{F_n} + \\frac{B}{F_{n+1} + F_n}.\\]\n\nTo find the values of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we combine the fractions on the right-hand side:\n\\[\\frac{A (F_{n+1} + F_n) + B F_n}{F_n (F_{n+1} + F_n)} = \\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n (F_{n+1} + F_n)}.\\]\n\nEquating the numerators, we get:\n\\[A (F_{n+1} + F_n) + B F_n = F_{n+1}.\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients, we obtain:\n\\[A = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad A + B = 0.\\]\n\nSince \\(A = 1\\), we have \\(B = -1\\). Therefore, the general term can be written as:\n\\[\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n (F_{n+1} + F_n)} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+1} + F_n} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+2}}.\\]\n\nThus, the series becomes a telescoping series:\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+2}} \\right).\\]\n\nIn a telescoping series, most terms cancel out. The series simplifies to:\n\\[\\left( \\frac{1}{F_1} - \\frac{1}{F_3} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_2} - \\frac{1}{F_4} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{F_3} - \\frac{1}{F_5} \\right) + \\cdots.\\]\n\nThe terms \\(\\frac{1}{F_3}, \\frac{1}{F_4}, \\frac{1}{F_5}, \\ldots\\) cancel out, leaving us with:\n\\[\\frac{1}{F_1} + \\frac{1}{F_2} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} = 2.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Function to generate Fibonacci numbers\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    fib = [0, 1]\n    for i in range(2, n+1):\n        fib.append(fib[-1] + fib[-2])\n    return fib\n\n# Generate the first few Fibonacci numbers\nfib_numbers = fibonacci(100)\n\n# Calculate the sum of the series up to a large number of terms\nsum_series = sum(fib_numbers[n+1] / (fib_numbers[n] * fib_numbers[n+2]) for n in range(1, 100))\n\nprint(sum_series)\n```", "Let's solve the problem step by step. We will use a Venn diagram approach to visualize the problem and set up the equations to solve for the unknowns.\n\n1. Let \\( C \\) be the set of students taking calculus.\n2. Let \\( P \\) be the set of students taking physics.\n3. Let \\( H \\) be the set of students taking chemistry.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- Total number of students, \\( N = 360 \\).\n- Number of students taking all three subjects, \\( |C \\cap P \\cap H| = 15 \\).\n- Number of students taking none of the subjects, \\( N - |C \\cup P \\cup H| = 15 \\). So, \\( |C \\cup P \\cup H| = 345 \\).\n- Number of students taking calculus, \\( |C| = 180 \\).\n- Number of students taking both calculus and chemistry, \\( |C \\cap H| = 75 \\).\n- Number of students taking both physics and chemistry, \\( |P \\cap H| = 75 \\).\n- Number of students taking both physics and calculus, \\( |C \\cap P| = 30 \\).\n- Twice as many students take chemistry as take physics, \\( |H| = 2|P| \\).\n\nWe need to find the number of students taking physics, \\( |P| \\).\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion-exclusion for three sets, we have:\n\\[\n|C \\cup P \\cup H| = |C| + |P| + |H| - |C \\cap P| - |C \\cap H| - |P \\cap H| + |C \\cap P \\cap H|\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n345 = 180 + |P| + 2|P| - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side:\n\\[\n345 = 180 + 3|P| - 165 + 15\n\\]\n\\[\n345 = 30 + 3|P|\n\\]\nSolving for \\( |P| \\):\n\\[\n315 = 3|P|\n\\]\n\\[\n|P| = 105\n\\]\n\nSo, the number of students taking physics is \\( \\boxed{105} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_none = 15\nstudents_all_three = 15\nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_calculus_chemistry = 75\nstudents_physics_chemistry = 75\nstudents_calculus_physics = 30\n\n# Number of students taking at least one subject\nstudents_at_least_one = total_students - students_none\n\n# Using the inclusion-exclusion principle\n# students_at_least_one = students_calculus + students_physics + students_chemistry - students_calculus_physics - students_calculus_chemistry - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n# Let students_physics = P and students_chemistry = 2 * P\n# students_at_least_one = students_calculus + P + 2 * P - students_calculus_physics - students_calculus_chemistry - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n# 345 = 180 + P + 2 * P - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n# 345 = 180 + 3 * P - 165 + 15\n# 345 = 30 + 3 * P\n# 315 = 3 * P\n# P = 105\n\nstudents_physics = (students_at_least_one - students_calculus + students_calculus_physics + students_calculus_chemistry + students_physics_chemistry - students_all_three) // 3\nprint(students_physics)\n```", "We can solve this problem by setting up a system of equations based on the given information and using the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\nLet's define the following variables:\n- \\( C \\) = number of students taking calculus\n- \\( P \\) = number of students taking physics\n- \\( Ch \\) = number of students taking chemistry\n- \\( C \\cap P \\) = number of students taking both calculus and physics\n- \\( C \\cap Ch \\) = number of students taking both calculus and chemistry\n- \\( P \\cap Ch \\) = number of students taking both physics and chemistry\n- \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch \\) = number of students taking all three subjects\n- \\( N \\) = total number of students\n\nGiven:\n- \\( N = 360 \\)\n- \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 15 \\)\n- Number of students taking none of the subjects = 15\n- \\( C = 180 \\)\n- \\( Ch = 2P \\)\n- \\( C \\cap Ch = 75 \\)\n- \\( P \\cap Ch = 75 \\)\n- \\( C \\cap P = 30 \\)\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion for three sets, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is:\n\\[ C + P + Ch - (C \\cap P + C \\cap Ch + P \\cap Ch) + C \\cap P \\cap Ch \\]\n\nThe number of students taking at least one of the subjects is:\n\\[ 360 - 15 = 345 \\]\n\nSo we can write:\n\\[ 180 + P + 2P - (30 + 75 + 75) + 15 = 345 \\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\\[ 180 + 3P - 165 + 15 = 345 \\]\n\\[ 30 + 3P = 345 \\]\n\\[ 3P = 315 \\]\n\\[ P = 105 \\]\n\nSo the number of students taking physics is \\( \\boxed{105} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\r\ntotal_students = 360\r\nstudents_none = 15\r\nstudents_C = 180\r\nstudents_C_and_P_and_Ch = 15\r\nstudents_C_and_Ch = 75\r\nstudents_P_and_Ch = 75\r\nstudents_C_and_P = 30\r\n\r\n# Let P be the number of students taking physics\r\n# Let Ch be the number of students taking chemistry\r\n# Ch = 2 * P\r\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\r\n# Students taking at least one subject = total_students - students_none\r\nstudents_at_least_one = total_students - students_none\r\n\r\n# Equation: students_C + P + Ch - (students_C_and_P + students_C_and_Ch + students_P_and_Ch) + students_C_and_P_and_Ch = students_at_least_one\r\n# Substitute Ch = 2 * P\r\n# 180 + P + 2 * P - (30 + 75 + 75) + 15 = 345\r\n# 180 + 3 * P - 165 + 15 = 345\r\n# 30 + 3 * P = 345\r\n# 3 * P = 315\r\n# P = 105\r\n\r\n# Solve for P\r\nP = (students_at_least_one - students_C + (students_C_and_P + students_C_and_Ch + students_P_and_Ch) - students_C_and_P_and_Ch) / 3\r\nprint(P)\n```", "Let's denote the number of students taking physics as $P$, the number of students taking calculus as $C$, and the number of students taking chemistry as $Ch$. We are given the following information:\n\n1. The total number of students is 360.\n2. 15 students take all three subjects.\n3. 15 students don't take any of the subjects.\n4. 180 students take calculus.\n5. Twice as many students take chemistry as take physics.\n6. 75 students take both calculus and chemistry.\n7. 75 students take both physics and chemistry.\n8. 30 students take both physics and calculus.\n\nFrom the information given, we can set up the following equations:\n\n1. The number of students taking calculus, physics, or chemistry is $360 - 15 = 345$.\n2. The number of students taking calculus is $C = 180$.\n3. The number of students taking chemistry is $Ch = 2P$.\n4. The number of students taking both calculus and chemistry is $C \\cap Ch = 75$.\n5. The number of students taking both physics and chemistry is $P \\cap Ch = 75$.\n6. The number of students taking both physics and calculus is $P \\cap C = 30$.\n7. The number of students taking all three subjects is $C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 15$.\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion-exclusion for three sets, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is given by:\n\\[ |C \\cup P \\cup Ch| = |C| + |P| + |Ch| - |C \\cap P| - |C \\cap Ch| - |P \\cap Ch| + |C \\cap P \\cap Ch| \\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 345 = 180 + P + 2P - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15 \\]\nSimplifying the equation:\n\\[ 345 = 180 + 3P - 150 + 15 \\]\n\\[ 345 = 45 + 3P \\]\n\\[ 300 = 3P \\]\n\\[ P = 100 \\]\n\nSo the number of students taking physics is $P = 100$. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_no_subject = 15\nstudents_all_three = 15\nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_both_calculus_chemistry = 75\nstudents_both_physics_chemistry = 75\nstudents_both_physics_calculus = 30\n\n# Students taking at least one subject\nstudents_at_least_one_subject = total_students - students_no_subject\n\n# Let P be the number of students taking physics\n# Let Ch be the number of students taking chemistry, so Ch = 2P\n# Using the inclusion-exclusion principle\n# students_at_least_one_subject = students_calculus + P + 2P - students_both_physics_calculus - students_both_calculus_chemistry - students_both_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n# 345 = 180 + P + 2P - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n# 345 = 180 + 3P - 150 + 15\n# 345 = 45 + 3P\n# 300 = 3P\n# P = 100\n\nP = (students_at_least_one_subject - students_calculus + students_both_physics_calculus + students_both_calculus_chemistry + students_both_physics_chemistry - students_all_three) // 3\nprint(P)\n```\n```output\n100\n```\nThe number of students taking physics is \\(\\boxed{100}\\).", "Let's denote the number of students taking physics as \\( P \\), the number of students taking chemistry as \\( C \\), and the number of students taking calculus as \\( K \\). From the problem, we have the following information:\n\n1. The total number of students is 360.\n2. 15 students take all three subjects.\n3. 15 students do not take any of the subjects.\n4. 180 students take calculus.\n5. Twice as many students take chemistry as take physics, so \\( C = 2P \\).\n6. 75 students take both calculus and chemistry.\n7. 75 students take both physics and chemistry.\n8. 30 students take both physics and calculus.\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion for three sets, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is:\n\\[ |C \\cup P \\cup K| = |C| + |P| + |K| - |C \\cap P| - |C \\cap K| - |P \\cap K| + |C \\cap P \\cap K| \\]\n\nWe know that the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is \\( 360 - 15 = 345 \\). So we can write:\n\\[ 345 = C + P + K - 75 - 75 - 30 + 15 \\]\n\\[ 345 = C + P + K - 150 + 15 \\]\n\\[ 345 = C + P + K - 135 \\]\n\\[ 480 = C + P + K \\]\n\nSince \\( C = 2P \\) and \\( K = 180 \\), we can substitute these values into the equation:\n\\[ 480 = 2P + P + 180 \\]\n\\[ 480 = 3P + 180 \\]\n\\[ 300 = 3P \\]\n\\[ P = 100 \\]\n\nSo the number of students taking physics is \\( \\boxed{100} \\).", "Let's break down the problem step by step and use Python to find the solution.\n\n1. Let's define the variables:\n   - \\( C \\): Students taking calculus.\n   - \\( P \\): Students taking physics.\n   - \\( Ch \\): Students taking chemistry.\n   - \\( C \\cap P \\): Students taking both calculus and physics.\n   - \\( C \\cap Ch \\): Students taking both calculus and chemistry.\n   - \\( P \\cap Ch \\): Students taking both physics and chemistry.\n   - \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch \\): Students taking all three subjects.\n   - \\( N \\): Total number of students.\n   - \\( N_0 \\): Students not taking any of the subjects.\n\n2. Given:\n   - \\( N = 360 \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 15 \\)\n   - \\( N_0 = 15 \\)\n   - \\( C = 180 \\)\n   - \\( Ch = 2 \\times P \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap Ch = 75 \\)\n   - \\( P \\cap Ch = 75 \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap P = 30 \\)\n\n3. We need to find the number of students taking physics (\\( P \\)).\n\n4. Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion for three sets, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is:\n   \\[\n   C + P + Ch - (C \\cap P + C \\cap Ch + P \\cap Ch) + C \\cap P \\cap Ch\n   \\]\n   The number of students taking at least one of the subjects is \\( 360 - 15 = 345 \\).\n\n5. Substitute the known values into the equation:\n   \\[\n   180 + P + 2P - (30 + 75 + 75) + 15 = 345\n   \\]\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   180 + 3P - 165 + 15 = 345\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   30 + 3P = 345\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3P = 315\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P = 105\n   \\]\n\nSo, the number of students taking physics is \\( \\boxed{105} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_no_subject = 15\nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_all_three = 15\nstudents_calculus_and_chemistry = 75\nstudents_physics_and_chemistry = 75\nstudents_calculus_and_physics = 30\n\n# Let P be the number of students taking physics\n# Let Ch be the number of students taking chemistry\n# We know Ch = 2 * P\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\n# students_calculus + students_physics + students_chemistry - (students_calculus_and_physics + students_calculus_and_chemistry + students_physics_and_chemistry) + students_all_three = total_students - students_no_subject\n# 180 + P + 2P - (30 + 75 + 75) + 15 = 345\n# 180 + 3P - 165 + 15 = 345\n# 30 + 3P = 345\n# 3P = 315\n# P = 105\n\nstudents_physics = 315 // 3\nprint(students_physics)\n```", "Let's denote the number of students taking physics as \\( P \\), the number of students taking chemistry as \\( C \\), and the number of students taking calculus as \\( K \\). We are given the following information:\n\n1. The total number of students is 360.\n2. 15 students take calculus, physics, and chemistry.\n3. 15 students don't take any of the subjects.\n4. 180 students take calculus.\n5. Twice as many students take chemistry as take physics, so \\( C = 2P \\).\n6. 75 students take both calculus and chemistry.\n7. 75 students take both physics and chemistry.\n8. 30 students take both physics and calculus.\n\nWe can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to set up our equations. First, we know that the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is \\( 360 - 15 = 345 \\). The principle of inclusion and exclusion for three sets gives us:\n\n\\[ |C \\cup P \\cup K| = |C| + |P| + |K| - |C \\cap P| - |C \\cap K| - |P \\cap K| + |C \\cap P \\cap K| \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get:\n\n\\[ 345 = C + P + K - 75 - 75 - 30 + 15 \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[ 345 = C + P + K - 150 + 15 \\]\n\\[ 345 = C + P + K - 135 \\]\n\\[ 480 = C + P + K \\]\n\nSince \\( C = 2P \\) and \\( K = 180 \\), we can substitute these values in:\n\n\\[ 480 = 2P + P + 180 \\]\n\\[ 480 = 3P + 180 \\]\n\\[ 300 = 3P \\]\n\\[ P = 100 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students taking physics is \\( \\boxed{100} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\n# Given values\r\ntotal_students = 360\r\nstudents_no_subject = 15\r\nstudents_all_three = 15\r\nstudents_calculus = 180\r\nstudents_chemistry = 2 * 100  # Since C = 2P and we found P = 100\r\nstudents_physics = 100\r\nstudents_calculus_chemistry = 75\r\nstudents_physics_chemistry = 75\r\nstudents_calculus_physics = 30\r\n\r\n# Number of students taking at least one subject\r\nstudents_at_least_one_subject = total_students - students_no_subject\r\n\r\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\r\nstudents_at_least_one_subject_calculated = students_chemistry + students_physics + students_calculus - students_calculus_chemistry - students_physics_chemistry - students_calculus_physics + students_all_three\r\n\r\n# Verify if the calculated value matches the expected value\r\nprint((students_at_least_one_subject, students_at_least_one_subject_calculated, students_at_least_one_subject == students_at_least_one_subject_calculated))\n```", 'Let the number of students taking physics be $x$.  Then the number of students taking chemistry is $2x$.  We are given that $75$ take both physics and chemistry.  So the number of students taking physics or chemistry is $x + 2x - 75 = 3x - 75$.  We are also given that $15$ take all three subjects and $15$ take none.  So the number of students taking exactly one or two subjects is $360 - 15 - 15 = 330$.  We are also given that $180$ take calculus, $75$ take both calculus and chemistry, and $30$ take both physics and calculus.  So the number of students taking exactly one or two subjects is $330 - 75 - 30 = 225$.  So we have the equation $3x - 75 = 225$.  Solving for $x$, we get $x = \\boxed{100}$.', "Let's break down the problem step-by-step and use Python to ensure the accuracy of our solution.\n\n1. **Define Variables:**\n   - Let \\( C \\) be the number of students taking calculus.\n   - Let \\( P \\) be the number of students taking physics.\n   - Let \\( Ch \\) be the number of students taking chemistry.\n   - Let \\( C \\cap P \\) be the number of students taking both calculus and physics.\n   - Let \\( C \\cap Ch \\) be the number of students taking both calculus and chemistry.\n   - Let \\( P \\cap Ch \\) be the number of students taking both physics and chemistry.\n   - Let \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch \\) be the number of students taking all three subjects.\n   - Let \\( N \\) be the total number of students in the school.\n   - Let \\( N_0 \\) be the number of students not taking any of the three subjects.\n\n2. **Given Information:**\n   - \\( N = 360 \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 15 \\)\n   - \\( N_0 = 15 \\)\n   - \\( C = 180 \\)\n   - \\( Ch = 2P \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap Ch = 75 \\)\n   - \\( P \\cap Ch = 75 \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap P = 30 \\)\n\n3. **Calculate the Number of Students Taking at Least One Subject:**\n   - The number of students taking at least one subject is \\( N - N_0 = 360 - 15 = 345 \\).\n\n4. **Use the Principle of Inclusion and Exclusion:**\n   - The number of students taking at least one subject can also be expressed as:\n     \\[\n     C + P + Ch - (C \\cap P + C \\cap Ch + P \\cap Ch) + C \\cap P \\cap Ch\n     \\]\n     Substituting the known values:\n     \\[\n     180 + P + 2P - (30 + 75 + 75) + 15 = 345\n     \\]\n     Simplifying the equation:\n     \\[\n     180 + 3P - 165 + 15 = 345\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     30 + 3P = 345\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3P = 315\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     P = 105\n     \\]\n\nSo, the number of students taking physics is \\( \\boxed{105} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_not_taking_any = 15\nstudents_taking_calculus = 180\nstudents_taking_all_three = 15\nstudents_taking_calculus_and_chemistry = 75\nstudents_taking_physics_and_chemistry = 75\nstudents_taking_calculus_and_physics = 30\n\n# Number of students taking at least one subject\nstudents_taking_at_least_one = total_students - students_not_taking_any\n\n# Let P be the number of students taking physics\n# Let Ch be the number of students taking chemistry, Ch = 2P\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion:\n# students_taking_at_least_one = students_taking_calculus + P + 2P - (students_taking_calculus_and_physics + students_taking_calculus_and_chemistry + students_taking_physics_and_chemistry) + students_taking_all_three\n# 345 = 180 + P + 2P - (30 + 75 + 75) + 15\n# 345 = 180 + 3P - 165 + 15\n# 345 = 30 + 3P\n# 3P = 315\n# P = 105\n\n# Calculate the number of students taking physics\nstudents_taking_physics = (students_taking_at_least_one - students_taking_calculus + students_taking_calculus_and_physics + students_taking_calculus_and_chemistry + students_taking_physics_and_chemistry - students_taking_all_three) / 3\nprint(students_taking_physics)\n```", 'Let $p$ be the number of students taking physics, $c$ be the number of students taking chemistry, and $x$ be the number of students taking only physics.  We are given that $c=2p$, $75=p+180-30-x$, and $x+15+180-30-75+75+15=360$.  Solving this system of equations, we find that $p=\\boxed{120}$.\n\xa0 \xa0\\boxed{120}', 'Let $c$ be the number of students taking calculus, $p$ be the number of students taking physics, and $ch$ be the number of students taking chemistry.  Let $cp$ be the number of students taking both calculus and physics, $cch$ be the number of students taking both calculus and chemistry, $p$ be the number of students taking both physics and chemistry, and $cpch$ be the number of students taking all three.  Let $x$ be the number of students taking only physics.  We can make a Venn diagram like this:\n[asy]\ndraw(circle((0,0),1));\ndraw(circle((2,0),1));\ndraw(circle((1,1.7320508075688772),1));\nlabel("Calculus",(-1.25,0));\nlabel("Physics",(2.25,0));\nlabel("Chemistry",(1,2.1));\nlabel("cp",(0.5,0.5));\nlabel("cch",(1.5,1.05));\nlabel("p",(1,0.5));\nlabel("cpch",(1,0.15));\nlabel("c-x",(0.5,-0.5));\nlabel("p-x",(2.5,0.5));\nlabel("ch-x",(1,1.25));\nlabel("30",(0.5,0.5),NE);\nlabel("75",(1.5,1.05),NE);\nlabel("75",(1,0.5),NE);\nlabel("15",(1,0.15),NE);\nlabel("15",(0.5,-0.5),NE);\nlabel("180",(0.5,-0.5),NE);\nlabel("x",(2.5,0.5),NE);\n[/asy]\nWe can fill in some numbers:\n[asy]\ndraw(circle((0,0),1));\ndraw(circle((2,0),1));\ndraw(circle((1,1.7320508075688772),1));\nlabel("Calculus",(-1.25,0));\nlabel("Physics",(2.25,0));\nlabel("Chemistry",(1,2.1));\nlabel("cp",(0.5,0.5));\nlabel("cch",(1.5,1.05));\nlabel("p",(1,0.5));\nlabel("cpch",(1,0.15));\nlabel("c-x",(0.5,-0.5));\nlabel("p-x",(2.5,0.5));\nlabel("ch-x",(1,1.25));\nlabel("30",(0.5,0.5),NE);\nlabel("75",(1.5,1.05),NE);\nlabel("75",(1,0.5),NE);\nlabel("15",(1,0.15),NE);\nlabel("15",(0.5,-0.5),NE);\nlabel("180",(0.5,-0.5),NE);\nlabel("x",(2.5,0.5),NE);\nlabel("150",(0.5,-0.5),NE);\n[/asy]\nThe total number of people taking physics is $x+30+75+15=120+x$.  The total number of people taking chemistry is $150+75+15=240$.  The total number of people taking calculus is $180+30+75+15=300$.  The total number of people taking at least one of the three subjects is $180+150+240-30-75-75-15+15=300$.  The total number of people in the school is 360, so the number of people taking none of the three subjects is $360-300=60$.  Therefore, the number of people taking physics is $\\boxed{60}$.\n\nFind the characteristic polynomial of the matrix:\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n-5 & 0 & -8 \\\\\n0 & -8 & 0 \\\\\n-2 & -7 & 4 \\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ To find the characteristic polynomial of the matrix \\( A = \\begin{bmatrix} -5 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 4 \\end{bmatrix} \\), we need to compute the determinant of the matrix \\( A - \\lambda I \\), where \\( I \\) is the identity matrix and \\( \\lambda \\) is a scalar. The characteristic polynomial is given by \\( \\det(A - \\lambda I) \\).\n\nFirst, we form the matrix \\( A - \\lambda I \\):\n\\[\nA - \\lambda I = \\begin{bmatrix} -5 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 4 \\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} -5 - \\lambda & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -8 - \\lambda & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute the determinant of this matrix:\n\\[\n\\det(A - \\lambda I) = \\begin{vmatrix} -5 - \\lambda & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -8 - \\lambda & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe can expand this determinant along the second row, which has two zeros, making the calculation simpler:\n\\[\n\\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0 \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} 0 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix} - (-8 - \\lambda) \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} -5 - \\lambda & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix} + 0 \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} -5 - \\lambda & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 \\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\det(A - \\lambda I) = (-8 - \\lambda) \\begin{vmatrix} -5 - \\lambda & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute the 2x2 determinant:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} -5 - \\lambda & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix} = (-5 - \\lambda)(4 - \\lambda) - (-8)(-2) = (-5 - \\lambda)(4 - \\lambda) - 16.\n\\]\nExpanding the product, we get:\n\\[\n(-5 - \\lambda)(4 - \\lambda) = -20 + 5\\lambda - 4\\lambda + \\lambda^2 = \\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 20.\n\\]\nSo, the determinant is:\n\\[\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 20 - 16 = \\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 36.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression for the determinant, we have:\n\\[\n\\det(A - \\lambda I) = (-8 - \\lambda)(\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 36).\n\\]\n\nFinally, we expand this product:\n\\[\n(-8 - \\lambda)(\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 36) = -8\\lambda^2 - 8\\lambda + 288 - \\lambda^3 - \\lambda^2 + 36\\lambda = -\\lambda^3 - 9\\lambda^2 + 28\\lambda + 288.\n\\]\n\nThus, the characteristic polynomial of the matrix is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\lambda^3 - 9\\lambda^2 + 28\\lambda + 288}.\n\\]\n\nWhat is the $\\ell_2$ norm of the vector $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -\\frac{11}{4}, \\frac{11}{4}, -\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{13}{2}, \\frac{19}{4}, \\frac{13}{2}, -\\frac{37}{4}\\right)$? To find the \\(\\ell_2\\) norm of the vector \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -\\frac{11}{4}, \\frac{11}{4}, -\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{13}{2}, \\frac{19}{4}, \\frac{13}{2}, -\\frac{37}{4}\\right)\\), we need to compute the square root of the sum of the squares of its components. The formula for the \\(\\ell_2\\) norm of a vector \\((x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|_2 = \\sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \\cdots + x_n^2}\n\\]\n\nFor the given vector, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\left( \\frac{5}{2}, -\\frac{11}{4}, \\frac{11}{4}, -\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{13}{2}, \\frac{19}{4}, \\frac{13}{2}, -\\frac{37}{4} \\right) \\right\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{5}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( -\\frac{11}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{11}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( -\\frac{13}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{19}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{13}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( -\\frac{37}{4} \\right)^2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate each square:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{5}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( -\\frac{11}{4} \\right)^2 = \\frac{121}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{11}{4} \\right)^2 = \\frac{121}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( -\\frac{13}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{169}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{19}{4} \\right)^2 = \\frac{361}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{13}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{169}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( -\\frac{37}{4} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1369}{16}\n\\]\n\nNext, we sum these squares. To do this, we first convert all fractions to have a common denominator of 16:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{4} = \\frac{100}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{4}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{169}{4} = \\frac{676}{16}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add them together:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{100}{16} + \\frac{121}{16} + \\frac{121}{16} + \\frac{4}{16} + \\frac{676}{16} + \\frac{361}{16} + \\frac{676}{16} + \\frac{1369}{16} = \\frac{3332}{16} = 208.25\n\\]\n\nFinally, we take the square root of the sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{208.25} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3332}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3332}}{4} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{833}}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{833}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(\\ell_2\\) norm of the vector is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{833}}{2}}\n\\]\n\nFind all real solutions to the equation $\\frac{\\log (x-17)}{\\log (9)} + \\frac{\\log (16-23x)}{\\log (9)} = \\frac{\\log (14x-14)}{\\log (9)}$. To solve the equation \\(\\frac{\\log (x-17)}{\\log (9)} + \\frac{\\log (16-23x)}{\\log (9)} = \\frac{\\log (14x-14)}{\\log (9)}\\), we can start by eliminating the common denominator \\(\\log (9)\\) from all terms. This simplifies the equation to:\n\n\\[\n\\log (x-17) + \\log (16-23x) = \\log (14x-14)\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, specifically \\(\\log a + \\log b = \\log (ab)\\), we can combine the logarithms on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\log ((x-17)(16-23x)) = \\log (14x-14)\n\\]\n\nSince the', "Let's break down the problem step by step and use Python to ensure accuracy.\n\n1. Let's define the variables:\n   - \\( C \\) = number of students taking calculus\n   - \\( P \\) = number of students taking physics\n   - \\( Ch \\) = number of students taking chemistry\n   - \\( CP \\) = number of students taking both calculus and physics\n   - \\( CC \\) = number of students taking both calculus and chemistry\n   - \\( PC \\) = number of students taking both physics and chemistry\n   - \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch \\) = number of students taking all three subjects\n   - \\( N \\) = number of students not taking any of the subjects\n\n2. From the problem, we know:\n   - \\( C = 180 \\)\n   - \\( CC = 75 \\)\n   - \\( PC = 75 \\)\n   - \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 15 \\)\n   - \\( N = 15 \\)\n   - \\( Ch = 2P \\)\n   - The total number of students is 360.\n\n3. Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is:\n   \\[\n   C + P + Ch - CP - CC - PC + C \\cap P \\cap Ch\n   \\]\n   Since 15 students don't take any of the subjects, the number of students taking at least one subject is \\( 360 - 15 = 345 \\). So we have:\n   \\[\n   180 + P + 2P - CP - 75 - 75 + 15 = 345\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation:\n   \\[\n   180 + 3P - CP - 135 = 345\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3P - CP = 295\n   \\]\n   We need to find \\( CP \\). Since \\( CP \\) is the number of students taking both calculus and physics, and we don't have a direct value for it, we can use the fact that the number of students taking calculus but not physics or chemistry is \\( 180 - CP - CC + C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 180 - CP - 75 + 15 = 120 - CP \\). Similarly, the number of students taking physics but not calculus or chemistry is \\( P - CP - PC + C \\cap P \\cap Ch = P - CP - 75 + 15 = P - CP - 60 \\). And the number of students taking chemistry but not calculus or physics is \\( 2P - CC - PC + C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 2P - 75 - 75 + 15 = 2P - 135 \\). The sum of these three groups plus the students taking all three subjects and the students not taking any subjects should equal 360:\n   \\[\n   (120 - CP) + (P - CP - 60) + (2P - 135) + 15 + 15 = 360\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation:\n   \\[\n   120 - CP + P - CP - 60 + 2P - 135 + 30 = 360\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   15 + 3P - 2CP = 360\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3P - 2CP = 345\n   \\]\n   We now have two equations:\n   \\[\n   3P - CP = 295\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3P - 2CP = 345\n   \\]\n   Subtracting the first equation from the second:\n   \\[\n   (3P - 2CP) - (3P - CP) = 345 - 295\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -CP = 50\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   CP = 50\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( CP = 50 \\) back into the first equation:\n   \\[\n   3P - 50 = 295\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3P = 345\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P = 115\n   \\]\n   So the number of students taking physics is \\( P = 115 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\r\ntotal_students = 360\r\nstudents_not_taking_any = 15\r\nstudents_taki