sh, Camille can choose any one of the remaining 9 dishes (since Camille cannot order the same dish as Yann).\n\nTherefore, the total number of different combinations of meals is the product of the number of choices Yann has and the number of choices Camille has after Yann has chosen. This is calculated as \\(10 \\times 9\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to compute this:\n\n```python\n# Number of dishes on the menu\ntotal_dishes = 10\n\n# Yann can choose any one of the 10 dishes\nyann_choices = total_dishes\n\n# Camille can choose any one of the remaining 9 dishes after Yann has chosen\ncamille_choices = total_dishes - 1\n\n# Total number of different combinations of meals\ntotal_combinations = yann_choices * camille_choices\n\nprint(total_combinations)\n```\n```output\n90\n```\nThe total number of different combinations of meals Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish, is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the number of different combinations of meals Yann and Camille can order given that they refuse to order the same dish. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Yann can choose any of the 10 dishes from the menu.\n2. Once Yann has chosen a dish, Camille can choose from the remaining 9 dishes (since Camille cannot order the same dish as Yann).\n\nSo, the total number of different combinations of meals is the product of the number of choices Yann has and the number of choices Camille has after Yann has made his choice. This can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total combinations} = 10 \\times 9 = 90 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Number of dishes on the menu\ntotal_dishes = 10\n\n# Number of different combinations of meals\ncombinations = total_dishes * (total_dishes - 1)\nprint(combinations)\n```\n```output\n90\n```\nThe total number of different combinations of meals that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish, is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).", "To 