rc) = \\frac{2 \\tan 10^\\circ}{1 - \\tan^2 10^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\tan 40^\\circ = \\tan (3 \\cdot 10^\\circ) = \\frac{3 \\tan 10^\\circ - \\tan^3 10^\\circ}{1 - 3 \\tan^2 10^\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nLet's denote \\(\\tan 10^\\circ\\) by \\(t\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ = \\frac{2t}{1 - t^2}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\tan 40^\\circ = \\frac{3t - t^3}{1 - 3t^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can then compute \\(\\cot^2 20^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cot^2 40^\\circ\\) as:\n\n\\[\n\\cot^2 20^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{1 - t^2}{2t}\\right)^2 = \\frac{(1 - t^2)^2}{4t^2}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\cot^2 40^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{1 - 3t^2}{3t - t^3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{(1 - 3t^2)^2}{t^2 (3 - t^2)^2}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we can substitute these values back into the expression and simplify. Let's use Python to compute the exact values.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the angle in degrees\nangle_10 = 10\nangle_20 = 20\nangle_40 = 40\n\n# Compute the tangent values\ntan_10 = math.tan(math.radians(angle_10))\ntan_20 = math.tan(math.radians(angle_20))\ntan_40 = math.tan(math.radians(angle_40))\n\n# Compute the cotangent squared values\ncot_20_squared = (1 / tan_20) ** 2\ncot_40_squared = (1 / tan_40) ** 2\n\n# Compute the final expression\nresult = 3 + tan_10 ** 2 + cot_20_squared + cot_40_squared\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n18.34259884403291\n```\nThe computed value of the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\) is approximately \\(18.3426\\). However, since we are dealing with trigonometric functions and exact values, we should check if this value can be expressed as a simple integer.\n\nGiven the symmetry and the nature of trigonometric functions, it is likely that the exact value is an integer. Let's check if the exact value is an integer by examining the possible exact values.\n\nWe can use the fact that \\(\\tan 10^\\circ\\), \\(\\tan 20^\\circ\\), and \\(\\tan 40^\\circ\\) are related through trigonometric identities and that their squar