mplify the computation of high powers of the matrix.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution\n\n1. **Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$:**\n   The eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of $\\mathbf{A}$ are the roots of the characteristic polynomial $\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0$. For the matrix $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, the characteristic polynomial is:\n   \\[\n   \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0.\n   \\]\n   Solving this quadratic equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}i}{2}$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3}i}{2}$.\n\n2. **Diagonalize the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$:**\n   Let $\\mathbf{P}$ be the matrix of eigenvectors and $\\mathbf{D}$ be the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues. Then, $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}$. We can then use the property that $\\mathbf{A}^n = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^n \\mathbf{P}^{-1}$ to compute high powers of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\n3. **Compute the powers of the diagonal matrix $\\mathbf{D}$:**\n   Since $\\mathbf{D}$ is a diagonal matrix, $\\mathbf{D}^n$ is simply the diagonal matrix with the $n$-th powers of the eigenvalues on the diagonal.\n\n4. **Compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$:**\n   Using the diagonalization, we can compute these powers and then sum them up.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the matrix A\nA = sp.Matrix([[3, 7], [-1, -2]])\n\n# Compute the eigenvalues and eigenvectors\neigenvals = A.eigenvals()\neigenvecs = A.eigenvects()\n\n# Construct the matrix P of eigenvectors and the diagonal matrix D of eigenvalues\nP = sp.Matrix.hstack(eigenvecs[0][2][0], eigenvecs[1][2][0])\nD = sp.diag(eigenvals[eigenvecs[0][0]], eigenvals[eigenvecs[1][0]])\n\n# Compute the inverse of P\nP_inv = P.inv()\n\n# Function to compute A^n using