en by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of distinct arrangements} = \\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(n\\) is the total number of items, and \\(n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k\\) are the frequencies of the indistinguishable items.\n\nIn this case, we have 4 items in total (2 IMO gold medals and 2 IPhO gold medals), so the formula becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of distinct arrangements} = \\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Total number of items\nn = 4\n# Number of indistinguishable IMO gold medals\nn1 = 2\n# Number of indistinguishable IPhO gold medals\nn2 = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of distinct arrangements\ndistinct_arrangements = math.factorial(n) // (math.factorial(n1) * math.factorial(n2))\nprint(distinct_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe number of distinct arrangements of 2 indistinguishable IMO gold medals and 2 indistinguishable IPhO gold medals is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the number of distinct arrangements of 2 IMO gold medals and 2 IPhO gold medals. This is a combinatorial problem where we need to count the number of unique permutations of a multiset.\n\nThe formula to find the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(n\\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\(n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k\\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this case, we have 4 items in total (2 IMO gold medals and 2 IPhO gold medals), so \\(n = 4\\). The frequencies of the IMO and IPhO gold medals are both 2, so \\(n_1 = 2\\) and \\(n_2 = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct arrangements is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this by using Python to calculate it.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Total number of items\nn = 4\n# Frequencies of the distinct items\nn