tly between these lines, i.e., \\(-5 < x < 5\\) and \\(-4 < y < 4\\).\n\nSo, \\(x\\) can take any integer value from \\(-4\\) to \\(4\\) (excluding \\(-5\\) and \\(5\\)), which gives us \\(9\\) possible values for \\(x\\) (\\(-4, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\)). Similarly, \\(y\\) can take any integer value from \\(-3\\) to \\(3\\) (excluding \\(-4\\) and \\(4\\)), which gives us \\(7\\) possible values for \\(y\\) (\\(-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3\\)).\n\nThe total number of interior points is the product of these possible values for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[9 \\times 7 = 63\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code snippet to iterate through the possible integer coordinates and count the interior points.\n```python\n# Define the range for x and y coordinates\nx_range = range(-4, 5)  # This is equivalent to -4, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\ny_range = range(-3, 4)  # This is equivalent to -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3\n\n# Count the number of interior points\ninterior_points_count = len(x_range) * len(y_range)\nprint(interior_points_count)\n```\n```output\n63\n```\nThe number of points with integer coordinates strictly in the interior of the rectangular region is \\(\\boxed{63}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of points with integer coordinates that lie strictly inside the rectangular region defined by the vertices \\((5, 4)\\), \\((-5, 4)\\), \\((-5, -4)\\), and \\((5, -4)\\).\n\n1. **Identify the boundaries of the rectangle:**\n   - The x-coordinates range from \\(-5\\) to \\(5\\).\n   - The y-coordinates range from \\(-4\\) to \\(4\\).\n\n2. **Exclude the boundary points:**\n   - The points on the boundary (including the vertices) should not be counted.\n\n3. **Count the interior points:**\n   - The x-coordinates of the interior points range from \\(-4\\) to \\(4\\) (excluding \\(-5\\) and \\(5\\)).\n   - The y-coordinates of the interior points range from \\(-3\\) to \\(3\\) (excluding \\(-4\\) and \\(4\\)).\n\n4. **Calculate the number of interior