ualities\r\nsolution1 = solveset(inequality1, p, domain=S.Reals)\r\nsolution2 = solveset(inequality2, p, domain=S.Reals)\r\n\r\n# Find the intersection of the solutions\r\nfinal_solution = solution1.intersect(solution2)\r\nprint(final_solution)\n```\n```output\nInterval.Lopen(3/5, 8/3)\n```\nThe solution to the inequalities \\(0 \\ge 54p - 144\\) and \\(0 > 12 - 20p\\) is the interval \\(\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]\\).\n\nSo, the final answer in interval notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\( p \\) that satisfy both inequalities:\n\n1. \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\)\n2. \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\)\n\nLet's solve each inequality separately and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\n### Solving the first inequality: \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\)\n\n\\[\n0 \\ge 54p - 144\n\\]\n\nAdd 144 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n144 \\ge 54p\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 54:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{144}{54} \\ge p\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{3} \\ge p \\quad \\text{or} \\quad p \\le \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Solving the second inequality: \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\)\n\n\\[\n0 > 12 - 20p\n\\]\n\nSubtract 12 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n-12 > -20p\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by -20 (and reverse the inequality sign):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{20} < p \\quad \\text{or} \\quad p > \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\n### Combining the solutions\n\nWe need to find the intersection of the solutions \\( p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\) and \\( p > \\frac{3}{5} \\). This gives us the interval:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]\n\\]\n\nLet's use Python to confirm this solution.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve, S\n\n# Define the variable\np = symbols('p')\n\n# Define the inequalities\nineq1 = 0 >= 54*p - 144\nineq2 = 0 > 12 - 20*p\n\n# Solve the inequalities\nsol1 = solve(ineq1, p)\nsol2 = solve(ineq2, p)\n\n# Find the intersection of the solutions\nsolution = S.Intersection(S.Reals, sol1, sol2)\nprin