e the vector $(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})$. We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the properties of the cross product to simplify the expression.\n\nThe cross product is distributive over addition, so we can expand the expression as follows:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we know that $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = -(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b})$. Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + \\mathbf{0} = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we know that $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})$. Therefore:\n\\[\n2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) = 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can substitute the values into the expression:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{p