 (a + b) \\cdot h = 84\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of \\( \\triangle ACD \\). Since \\( \\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD} \\), the area of \\( \\triangle ACD \\) is:\n\\[\n[ \\triangle ACD ] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot AD \\cdot h = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot a \\cdot h\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( a \\cdot h \\), we can use the fact that \\( \\overline{AC} \\) bisects \\( \\angle BAD \\). This implies that the area of \\( \\triangle ACD \\) is half of the area of the trapezoid minus the area of \\( \\triangle ABC \\). Since \\( \\overline{AC} \\) is the height for both triangles \\( \\triangle ACD \\) and \\( \\triangle ABC \\), we can use the proportion of the bases \\( AD \\) and \\( BC \\) to find the area of \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\n\nSince \\( \\overline{AC} \\) bisects \\( \\angle BAD \\), the ratio of the areas of \\( \\triangle ACD \\) and \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is the same as the ratio of \\( AD \\) to \\( BC \\). Let's denote the area of \\( \\triangle ABC \\) as \\( x \\). Then the area of \\( \\triangle ACD \\) is \\( 42 - x \\). Since the ratio of the areas is the same as the ratio of the bases, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{[ \\triangle ACD ]}{[ \\triangle ABC ]} = \\frac{a}{b} \\implies \\frac{42 - x}{x} = \\frac{a}{b}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( (a + b) \\cdot h = 84 \\), we can use the fact that the total area of the trapezoid is the sum of the areas of \\( \\triangle ACD \\) and \\( \\triangle ABC \\):\n\\[\n42 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot a \\cdot h + x\n\\]\nSince \\( x = \\frac{b}{a+b} \\cdot 42 \\), we can substitute and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n42 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot a \\cdot h + \\frac{b}{a+b} \\cdot 42\n\\]\nSince \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot a \\cdot h = 42 - x \\), we can substitute and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n42 = (42 - x) + \\frac{b}{a+b} \\cdot 42\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{b}{a+b} \\cdot 42\n\\]\nSince \\( (a + b) \\cdot h = 84 \\), we can use the fact that the total area of the trapezoid is the sum of the areas of \\( \\triangle ACD \\) and \\( \\