x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the terms in a more convenient form:\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{-(a - b)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(a - c)(b - c)}. \\]\n\nWe can combine the terms over a common denominator:\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)(b - c) - (x - a)(x - c)(a - c) + (x - a)(x - b)(a - b)}{(a - b)(a - c)(b - c)}. \\]\n\nNow, let's expand the numerator:\n\\[ (x - b)(x - c)(b - c) = (x^2 - (b+c)x + bc)(b - c) = (b - c)x^2 - (b+c)(b-c)x + bc(b-c), \\]\n\\[ (x - a)(x - c)(a - c) = (x^2 - (a+c)x + ac)(a - c) = (a - c)x^2 - (a+c)(a-c)x + ac(a-c), \\]\n\\[ (x - a)(x - b)(a - b) = (x^2 - (a+b)x + ab)(a - b) = (a - b)x^2 - (a+b)(a-b)x + ab(a-b). \\]\n\nCombining these, the numerator becomes:\n\\[ (b - c)x^2 - (b+c)(b-c)x + bc(b-c) - (a - c)x^2 + (a+c)(a-c)x - ac(a-c) + (a - b)x^2 - (a+b)(a-b)x + ab(a-b). \\]\n\nGrouping the terms, we get:\n\\[ [(b - c) - (a - c) + (a - b)]x^2 + [-(b+c)(b-c) + (a+c)(a-c) - (a+b)(a-b)]x + [bc(b-c) - ac(a-c) + ab(a-b)]. \\]\n\nSimplifying the coefficients, we see that the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is:\n\\[ (b - c) - (a - c) + (a - b) = b - c - a + c + a - b = 0. \\]\n\nSo the polynomial simplifies to a linear expression. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is 1.\n\nLet's confirm this by evaluating the polynomial at specific values of \\(x\\). If we evaluate \\( p(x) \\) at \\( x = a \\), \\( x = b \\), and \\( x = c \\), we should get specific results that confirm the polynomial is linear.\n\n\\[ p(a) = \\frac{(a - b)(a - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\\[ p(b) = \\frac{(b - b)(b - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\\[ p(c) = \\frac{(c - b)(c - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - c)