\n1. \\( x - 4 = 12 \\)\n2. \\( x - 4 = -12 \\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\nFor the first case:\n\n\\[ x - 4 = 12 \\implies x = 16 \\]\n\nFor the second case:\n\n\\[ x - 4 = -12 \\implies x = -8 \\]\n\nSo the possible values of \\( x \\) are 16 and -8. The product of these values is:\n\n\\[ 16 \\times (-8) = -128 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the possible values of x\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the possible values of x\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the equation \\( |x-4| - 10 = 2 \\), we first isolate the absolute value expression. Adding 10 to both sides of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ |x-4| = 12 \\]\n\nThe absolute value equation \\( |x-4| = 12 \\) can be broken down into two separate linear equations:\n\n1. \\( x-4 = 12 \\)\n2. \\( x-4 = -12 \\)\n\nWe solve each equation separately:\n\n1. \\( x-4 = 12 \\)\n\\[ x = 12 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x = 16 \\]\n\n2. \\( x-4 = -12 \\)\n\\[ x = -12 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x = -8 \\]\n\nSo the possible values of \\( x \\) are 16 and -8. The product of these values is:\n\n\\[ 16 \\times (-8) = -128 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the possible values of x\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the possible values of x\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the equation \\( |x-4| - 10 = 2 \\), we first need to isolate the absolute value expression. Adding 10 to both sides of the equation gives us:\n\n\\[ |x-4| = 12 \\]\n\nThe absolute value equation \\( |x-4| = 12 \\) can be broken down into two separate linear equations:\n\n\\[ x-4 = 12 \\]\n\\[ x-4 = -12 \\]\n\nSolving these equations for \\( x \\) gives us:\n\n\\[ x = 16 \\]\n\\[ x = -8 \\]\n\nSo the possible values of \\( x \\) are 16 and -8. The product of these valu