600} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ m/s} \\]\n\nThe robot takes 90 seconds to travel the length of the hallway. Using the speed in meters per second, the length of the hallway is:\n\\[ \\text{Length of hallway} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ m/s} \\times 90 \\text{ s} = 10 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given data\ntime_to_travel_1_km = 2.5  # hours\ndistance_1_km = 1  # kilometers\ntime_to_travel_hallway = 90  # seconds\n\n# Calculate speed in km/h\nspeed_km_per_h = distance_1_km / time_to_travel_1_km\n\n# Convert speed to m/s\nspeed_m_per_s = speed_km_per_h * (1000 / 3600)\n\n# Calculate the length of the hallway in meters\nlength_of_hallway = speed_m_per_s * time_to_travel_hallway\nprint(length_of_hallway)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe length of the hallway is \\(\\boxed{10}\\) meters.", "To determine the length of the hallway, we need to calculate the distance the robot travels in 90 seconds at the same constant speed it travels 1 kilometer in 2.5 hours.\n\nFirst, let's find the speed of the robot in meters per second. We know that:\n\n1 kilometer = 1000 meters\n2.5 hours = 2.5 * 3600 seconds = 9000 seconds\n\nSo the speed of the robot is:\n\\[ \\text{Speed} = \\frac{1000 \\text{ meters}}{9000 \\text{ seconds}} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ meters per second} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find out how far the robot travels in 90 seconds at this speed:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ meters/second} \\times 90 \\text{ seconds} = 10 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nSo the length of the hallway is 10 meters. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Constants\ndistance_km = 1  # distance in kilometers\ntime_hours = 2.5  # time in hours\ntime_seconds = 90  # time in seconds for the hallway\n\n# Convert distance to meters and time to seconds\ndistance_meters = distance_km * 1000  # 1 kilometer = 1000 meters\ntime_hours_in_seconds = time_hours * 3600  # 1 hour = 3600 seconds\n\n# Calculate speed in meters per second\nspeed_m_per_s = distance_meters / time_hours_in_seconds\n\n# Calculate the length of the hallway\nhallway_length_meters = speed_m_per_s * time_seconds\nprint(hallway_length_meters)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe length of the hallway is \\(\\boxed{10}\\) meters.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the speed of the robot and then use that speed to find the length of the hallway.\n\n1. **Determine the speed of the robot:**\n   - The robot takes 2.5 hours to travel 1 kilometer.\n   - Speed is calculated as distance divided by time. So, the speed of the robot is \\( \\frac{1 \\text{ km}}{2.5 \\text{ hours}} \\).\n\n2. **Convert the speed to meters per second:**\n   - 1 kilometer is equal to 1000 meters.\n   - 2.5 hours is equal to \\( 2.5 \\times 3600 = 9000 \\) seconds.\n   - So, the speed of the robot is \\( \\frac{1000 \\text{ meters}}{9000 \\text{ seconds}} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ meters per second} \\).\n\n3. **Determine the length of the hallway:**\n   - The robot takes 90 seconds to travel the length of the hallway.\n   - Using the speed of the robot, the length of the hallway is \\( \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\frac{1}{9} \\text{ meters/second} \\times 90 \\text{ seconds} \\).\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the speed of the robot in meters per second\nspeed_m_per_s = 1000 / (2.5 * 3600)\n\n# Define the time taken to travel the length of the hallway in seconds\ntime_seconds = 90\n\n# Calculate the length of the hallway in meters\nhallway_length_m = speed_m_per_s * time_seconds\nprint(hallway_length_m)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe length of the hallway is \\(\\boxed{100}\\) meters.", "To solve the problem, we need to find the value of \\( b \\) such that \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\). Let's break down the problem step-by-step.\n\n1. First, we need to find \\( q(1) \\). Since \\( q(x) = bx + 1 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1\n   \\]\n\n2. Next, we need to find \\( f(q(1)) \\). Since \\( f(x) = x - 3 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b - 2\n   \\]\n\n3. We are given that \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\), so we can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   b - 2 = -3\n   \\]\n\n4. Solving for \\( b \\):\n   \\[\n   b = -3 + 2 = -1\n   \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( b \\) is \\(-1\\). Let's confirm this by implementing the steps in Python.\n```python\n# Define the functions f and q\ndef f(x):\n    return x - 3\n\ndef q(x, b):\n    return b * x + 1\n\n# Find q(1)\nq_1 = q(1, b)  # We will use b = -1 to confirm our solution\n\n# Find f(q(1))\nf_q_1 = f(q_1)\n\n# Check if f(q(1)) is -3\nprint(f_q_1)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'b' is not defined\n```\nIt seems I didn't properly define the variable `b` in the code. Let's fix that by explicitly defining `b` and then running the calculations again.\n```python\n# Define the functions f and q\ndef f(x):\n    return x - 3\n\ndef q(x, b):\n    return b * x + 1\n\n# Define the value of b\nb = -1\n\n# Find q(1)\nq_1 = q(1, b)\n\n# Find f(q(1))\nf_q_1 = f(q_1)\n\n# Check if f(q(1)) is -3\nprint(f_q_1)\n```\n```output\n-3\n```\nThe calculation confirms that when \\( b = -1 \\), \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\). Therefore, the value of \\( b \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).", "To solve for \\( b \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\( q(1) \\).\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\).\n3. Set the result equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\( b \\).\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n1. Calculate \\( q(1) \\):\n\\[ q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b - 2 \\]\n\n3. Set the result equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[ b - 2 = -3 \\]\n\\[ b = -3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ b = -1 \\]\n\nLet's verify this by coding the solution in Python.\n```python\n# Define the functions f and q\ndef f(x):\n    return x - 3\n\ndef q(x, b):\n    return b * x + 1\n\n# Calculate q(1)\nq_1 = q(1, b)\n\