ot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b + 1 - 3 = b - 2 \\]\n\nWe are given that \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\), so:\n\\[ b - 2 = -3 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( b \\):\n\\[ b = -3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ b = -1 \\]\n\nLet's verify this by substituting \\( b = -1 \\) back into the equations.\n\n1. Calculate \\( q(1) \\) with \\( b = -1 \\):\n\\[ q(1) = -1 \\cdot 1 + 1 = -1 + 1 = 0 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) = 0 \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(0) = 0 - 3 = -3 \\]\n\nSince \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\) when \\( b = -1 \\), the solution is correct. The value of \\( b \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).", "To solve for \\( b \\) in the given problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Evaluate \\( q(1) \\) using the function \\( q(x) = bx + 1 \\).\n2. Substitute the result of \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\).\n3. Set the result equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\( b \\).\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step.\n\n1. Evaluate \\( q(1) \\):\n\\[ q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b - 2 \\]\n\n3. Set the result equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[ b - 2 = -3 \\]\n\\[ b = -3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ b = -1 \\]\n\nSo the value of \\( b \\) is \\(-1\\). Let's confirm this by implementing the steps in Python.\n```python\n# Define the functions f and q\ndef f(x):\n    return x - 3\n\ndef q(x, b):\n    return b * x + 1\n\n# Given that f(q(1)) = -3, we need to find the value of b\n# First, calculate q(1)\nx = 1\nb = -1  # We will verify this value\nq_1 = q(x, b)\n\n# Now calculate f(q(1))\nf_q_1 = f(q_1)\n\n# Check if f(q(1)) is equal to -3\nprint((f_q_1, b))\n```\n```output\n(-3, -1)\n```\nThe calculation confirms that when \\( b = -1 \\), \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\). Therefore, the value of \\( b \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).", "Given the functions \\( f(x) = x - 3 \\) and \\( q(x) = bx + 1 \\), we need to find the value of \\( b \\) such that \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\).\n\nFirst,