 we get:\n\\[19x - 4x = 15x.\\]\n\nThe constant terms are \\(1\\) and \\(-81\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[1 - 81 = -80.\\]\n\nSo the simplified expression is:\n\\[15x - 80.\\]\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this simplification.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpression = 19*x + 1 - 4*x - 81\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expression = simplify(expression)\r\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n15*x - 80\n```\nThe simplified expression is \\(\\boxed{15x - 80}\\).", "To simplify the expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\), we need to combine like terms. Specifically, we combine the \\(x\\) terms and the constant terms separately.\n\nThe \\(x\\) terms are \\(19x\\) and \\(-4x\\), and the constant terms are \\(1\\) and \\(-81\\). Combining these gives:\n\\[19x - 4x + 1 - 81 = (19 - 4)x + (1 - 81) = 15x - 80.\\]\n\nSo, the simplified expression is \\(15x - 80\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this:\n\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = 19*x + 1 - 4*x - 81\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = simplify(expression)\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n15*x - 80\n```\nThe simplified expression is \\(\\boxed{15x - 80}\\).", "To simplify the expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\), we need to combine like terms. Specifically, we combine the \\(x\\) terms and the constant terms separately.\n\nThe \\(x\\) terms are \\(19x\\) and \\(-4x\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[19x - 4x = 15x.\\]\n\nThe constant terms are \\(1\\) and \\(-81\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[1 - 81 = -80.\\]\n\nSo the simplified expression is:\n\\[15x - 80.\\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\n