gers in this range.\n```python\n# Calculate the range of integers that satisfy the inequality -3.6 <= x <= 7.6\nstart = int(-3.6)  # This will give -3\nend = int(7.6)     # This will give 7\n\n# List of integers in the range\nintegers_in_range = list(range(start, end + 1))\n\n# Number of integers in the range\nnum_integers = len(integers_in_range)\nprint((num_integers, integers_in_range))\n```\n```output\n(11, [-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\n```\nThe integers in the range \\(-3.6 \\leq x \\leq 7.6\\) are \\(-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\\). Therefore, the number of integers in the solution set is \\(11\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]", "The absolute value inequality \\( |x-2| \\leq 5.6 \\) can be rewritten as:\n\\[ -5.6 \\leq x-2 \\leq 5.6 \\]\n\nTo isolate \\( x \\), we add 2 to all parts of the inequality:\n\\[ -5.6 + 2 \\leq x \\leq 5.6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ -3.6 \\leq x \\leq 7.6 \\]\n\nThe integers within the range from \\(-3.6\\) to \\(7.6\\) are:\n\\[ -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 \\]\n\nSo, we need to count these integers. Let's use Python to ensure the count is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the range of integers\nstart = -3\nend = 7\n\n# List all integers in the range and count them\nintegers_in_range = list(range(start, end + 1))\nnumber_of_integers = len(integers_in_range)\nprint(number_of_integers)\n```\n```output\n11\n```\nThe number of integers in the solution set of \\( |x-2| \\leq 5.6 \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).", "To determine the number of integers in the solution set of the inequality \\( |x - 2| \\leq 5.6 \\), we first need to understand what the inequality represents.\n\nThe inequality \\( |x - 2| \\leq 5.6 \\) means that the distance between \\( x \\) and 2 on the number line is less than or equal to 5.6. This can be rewritten as:\n\\[ -5.6 \\leq x - 2 \\leq 5.6 \\]\nBy adding 2 to all parts of the inequality, we get:\n\\[ -5.6 + 2 \\leq x \\leq 5.6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ -3.6 \\leq x \\leq 7.6 \\]\n\nThis means \\( x \\) 