Jen}} \\cdot a_{\\text{Jen}} = m_{\\text{Jack}} \\cdot a_{\\text{Jack}} \\]\n\n3. We are given the masses of the softball and the baseball:\n\\[ m_{\\text{Jen}} = 200 \\text{ g} \\]\n\\[ m_{\\text{Jack}} = 150 \\text{ g} \\]\n\n4. We need to find the ratio of the acceleration of Jen's ball to Jack's ball, which is:\n\\[ \\frac{a_{\\text{Jen}}}{a_{\\text{Jack}}} \\]\n\n5. From the equation \\( m_{\\text{Jen}} \\cdot a_{\\text{Jen}} = m_{\\text{Jack}} \\cdot a_{\\text{Jack}} \\), we can solve for the ratio:\n\\[ \\frac{a_{\\text{Jen}}}{a_{\\text{Jack}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{Jack}}}{m_{\\text{Jen}}} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{a_{\\text{Jen}}}{a_{\\text{Jack}}} = \\frac{150}{200} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{a_{\\text{Jen}}}{a_{\\text{Jack}}} = \\frac{3}{4} \\]\n\nSo, the ratio of the acceleration of Jen's ball to Jack's ball is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given masses\nmass_jen = 200  # in grams\nmass_jack = 150  # in grams\n\n# Calculate the ratio of accelerations\nratio = mass_jack / mass_jen\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nsimplified_ratio = Fraction(mass_jack, mass_jen)\n\nprint(simplified_ratio)\n```\n```output\n3/4\n```\nThe ratio of the acceleration of Jen's ball to Jack's ball is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\).", "First, let's convert the masses of the softball and the baseball from grams to kilograms, since the standard unit for mass in the formula \\( F = ma \\) is kilograms.\n\n1. The mass of the softball is \\( 200 \\) g, which is \\( 0.2 \\) kg.\n2. The mass of the baseball is \\( 150 \\) g, which is \\( 0.15 \\) kg.\n\nSince the force \\( F \\) is the same for both balls, we can use the formula \\( F = ma \\) to find the ratio of the accelerations. Let's denote the acceleration of the softball as \\( a_s \\) and the acceleration of the baseball as \\( a_b \\). The force \\( F \\) is the same for both, so we can write:\n\n\\[ F = m_s a_s = m_b a_b \\]\n\nSince the force \\( F \\) is the same, we can set the two 