its[::-1])\n\n# Convert 629 to base 7 and base 8\nbase_7_representation = decimal_to_base(629, 7)\nbase_8_representation = decimal_to_base(629, 8)\n\n# Find the common digits\ncommon_digits = set(base_7_representation).intersection(base_8_representation)\ncommon_digits_count = len(common_digits)\n\nprint((base_7_representation, base_8_representation, common_digits_count))\n```\n```output\n(\'1556\', \'1165\', 3)\n```\nThe base 7 representation of \\(629_{10}\\) is \\(1556_7\\) and the base 8 representation is \\(1165_8\\). The common digits between these two representations are \\(1, 5, 6\\), so there are 3 digits that are the same in both representations.\n\nThus, the number of the same digits in the base 7 and base 8 representations of \\(629_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).', "To determine how many of the same digits are found in the base 7 and base 8 representations of \\(629_{10}\\), we first need to convert \\(629_{10}\\) to its base 7 and base 8 representations.\n\n### Step 1: Convert \\(629_{10}\\) to base 7\n\nTo convert a decimal number to base 7, we repeatedly divide the number by 7 and keep track of the remainders.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n629 \\div 7 &= 89 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\\\\n89 \\div 7 &= 12 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\\\\n12 \\div 7 &= 1 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\\\\n1 \\div 7 &= 0 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get \\(629_{10} = 1556_7\\).\n\n### Step 2: Convert \\(629_{10}\\) to base 8\n\nTo convert a decimal number to base 8, we repeatedly divide the number by 8 and keep track of the remainders.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n629 \\div 8 &= 78 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\\\\n78 \\div 8 &= 9 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\\\\n9 \\div 8 &= 1 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\\\\n1 \\div 8 &= 0 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get \\(629_{10} = 1165_8\\).\n\n### Step 3: Compare the digits of \\(1556_7\\) and \\(1165_8\\)\n\nThe digits in \\(1556_7\\) are \\{1, 5, 6\\} and the digits in \\(1165_8\\) are \\{1, 6, 5\\}. The common digits are \\{1, 5, 6\\}, so there are 3 common digits.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).\n\nHere is the Python code to perform these steps and verify the result:\n\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base(n, base):\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        digits.append(int(n % base))\n        n //= base\n    return ''.join(str(x) for x in digits[::-1])\n\n# Convert 629 to base 7 and base 8\nbase_7_representation = decimal_to_base(629, 7)\nbase_8_representation = decimal_to_base(629, 8)\n\n# Find the common digits\ncommon_digits = set(base_7_representation).intersection(set(base_8_representation))\ncommon_digit_count = len(common_digits)\n\nprint(common_digit_count)\n```", 'To determine how many of the same digits are found in the base 7 and base 8 representations of the decimal number 629, we need to first convert 629 from base 10 to base 7 and base 8. Then, we will compare the digits of the two representations.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Convert 629 from base 10 to base 7:**\n   - Divide 629 by 7 and record the remainder.\n   - Divide the quotient by 7 and record the remainder.\n   - Repeat until the quotient is 0.\n   - The base 7 representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\n2. **Convert 629 from base 10 to base 8:**\n   - Divide 629 by 8 and record the remainder.\n   - Divide the quotient by 8 and record the remainder.\n   - Repeat until the quotient is 0.\n   - The base 8 representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\n3. **Compare the digits of the base 7 and base 8 representations:**\n   - Convert the base 7 and base 8 representations to strings.\n   - Find the intersection of the digits in the two strings.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python to get the accurate result.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base(n, base):\n    """Convert a decimal number to a given base and return the string representation."""\n    if n == 0:\n        r