rccos 3x.\n\\]\nFrom the given equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\theta_1 + \\theta_2 + \\theta_3 = \\pi.\n\\]\nUsing the identity \\(\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2 + \\theta_3) = \\cos \\pi = -1\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2 + \\theta_3) = \\cos \\theta_1 \\cos \\theta_2 \\cos \\theta_3 - \\cos \\theta_1 \\sin \\theta_2 \\sin \\theta_3 - \\sin \\theta_1 \\cos \\theta_2 \\sin \\theta_3 - \\sin \\theta_1 \\sin \\theta_2 \\cos \\theta_3 = -1.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(\\cos \\theta_1 = x\\), \\(\\cos \\theta_2 = 2x\\), \\(\\cos \\theta_3 = 3x\\), and using the identity \\(\\sin \\theta = \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\\), we get:\n\\[\nx \\cdot 2x \\cdot 3x - x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot 2x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\cdot 3x = -1.\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n6x^3 - x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2} - 2x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2} - 3x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} = -1.\n\\]\nDividing through by \\(x\\) (assuming \\(x \\neq 0\\)), we get:\n\\[\n6x^2 - \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2} - 2 \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2} - 3 \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} = -\\frac{1}{x}.\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square roots, we can square both sides of the equation, but this will lead to a complicated expression. Instead, we can use numerical methods to find the value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the original equation and then derive the cubic polynomial.\n\nUsing Python, we can find the value of \\(x\\) numerically and then use polynomial fitting to find the coefficients of the cubic polynomial. Let's do this step-by-step.\n\nFirst, we will find the numerical value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi\\). Then, we will use polynomial fitting to find the coefficients of the cubic polynomial.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import fsolve\n\n# Defin