thon to ensure accuracy.\n\nFirst, we will perform the division and then check if the result is a terminating decimal. A fraction \\(\\frac{a}{b}\\) in its simplest form is a terminating decimal if and only if the prime factorization of the denominator \\(b\\) (after simplifying the fraction) contains no prime factors other than 2 and 5.\n\nLet's start by performing the division and checking the prime factorization of the denominator.\n```python\nfrom fractions import Fraction\r\n\r\n# Define the fraction\r\nfraction = Fraction(57, 160)\r\n\r\n# Perform the division\r\ndecimal_value = fraction.limit_denominator().numerator / fraction.limit_denominator().denominator\r\n\r\n# Check the prime factorization of the denominator\r\ndenominator = fraction.limit_denominator().denominator\r\nprime_factors = []\r\n\r\n# Factor out the 2s\r\nwhile denominator % 2 == 0:\r\n    prime_factors.append(2)\r\n    denominator //= 2\r\n\r\n# Factor out the 5s\r\nwhile denominator % 5 == 0:\r\n    prime_factors.append(5)\r\n    denominator //= 5\r\n\r\n# If the denominator is 1 after factoring out 2s and 5s, it is a terminating decimal\r\nis_terminating = denominator == 1\r\n\r\nprint((decimal_value, is_terminating))\n```\n```output\n(0.35625, True)\n```\nThe fraction \\(\\frac{57}{160}\\) converts to the decimal 0.35625, and the prime factorization of the denominator (160) contains only the factors 2 and 5. This confirms that the decimal is terminating.\n\nTherefore, the terminating decimal representation of \\(\\frac{57}{160}\\) is \\(\\boxed{0.35625}\\).", "To convert the fraction $\\frac{57}{160}$ to a terminating decimal, we can perform the division of 57 by 160. Let's do this step by step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Perform the division to convert the fraction to a decimal\nfraction = 57 / 160\nprint(fraction)\n```\n```output\n0.35625\n```\nThe fraction $\\frac{57}{160}$ converts to the terminating decimal $0.35625$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxe