 integer solution to the congruence is \\(u = 39\\). The next smallest positive integer solution can be found by adding the modulus (50) to 39, which gives \\(u = 89\\).\n\nThe average of the two smallest positive integer solutions is:\n\n\\[\\frac{39 + 89}{2} = \\frac{128}{2} = 64\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find modular inverse using Extended Euclidean Algorithm\ndef modular_inverse(a, m):\n    m0, x0, x1 = m, 0, 1\n    if m == 1:\n        return 0\n    while a > 1:\n        # q is quotient\n        q = a // m\n        m, a = a % m, m\n        x0, x1 = x1 - q * x0, x0\n    # Make x1 positive\n    if x1 < 0:\n        x1 += m0\n    return x1\n\n# Given values\na = 14\nb = 46\nm = 100\n\n# Simplify the congruence by dividing by gcd(14, 2)\na_simplified = a // 2\nb_simplified = b // 2\nm_simplified = m // 2\n\n# Find the modular inverse of 7 modulo 50\ninverse = modular_inverse(a_simplified, m_simplified)\n\n# Find the smallest positive integer solution\nu1 = (b_simplified * inverse) % m_simplified\n\n# The next smallest positive integer solution\nu2 = u1 + m_simplified\n\n# Calculate the average of the two smallest positive integer solutions\naverage = (u1 + u2) // 2\n\nprint((u1, u2, average))\n```\n```output\n(39, 89, 64)\n```\nThe two smallest positive integer solutions to the congruence \\(14u \\equiv 46 \\pmod{100}\\) are \\(u = 39\\) and \\(u = 89\\). The average of these two solutions is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]", "To solve the congruence \\(14u \\equiv 46 \\pmod{100}\\), we need to find the values of \\(u\\) that satisfy this equation. This can be done by finding the modular inverse of 14 modulo 100, if it exists, and then using it to solve for \\(u\\).\n\nFirst, we need to check if 14 and 100 are coprime, i.e., if their greatest common divisor (gcd) is 1. If they are coprime, then the modular inverse of 14 modulo 100 exists. We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the modular inverse.\n\