 \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the distance between the two buildings on the map given that 3 inches on the map equates to 10 miles in real life, and the real-life distance between the two buildings is 53.25 miles.\n\nFirst, we can find out how many inches on the map correspond to 1 mile in real life. Since 3 inches on the map is equivalent to 10 miles in real life, we can set up the following ratio:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance on the map (in inches)} = \\left(\\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}}\\right) \\times \\text{Real-life distance (in miles)} \\]\n\nGiven that the real-life distance is 53.25 miles, we can substitute this value into the equation:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance on the map (in inches)} = \\left(\\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}}\\right) \\times 53.25 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance on the map (in inches)} = \\frac{3 \\times 53.25}{10} \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nLet's calculate this value using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\nmap_distance_per_10_miles = 3  # inches\nreal_life_distance = 53.25  # miles\n\n# Calculate the map distance for the given real-life distance\nmap_distance = (map_distance_per_10_miles / 10) * real_life_distance\nprint(map_distance)\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe distance between the two buildings on the map is 15.975 inches. To express this as a fraction, we need to convert 15.975 to a fraction.\n\nFirst, we can express 15.975 as a fraction by recognizing that 15.975 is the same as \\(15 + \\frac{975}{1000}\\). Simplifying \\(\\frac{975}{1000}\\) gives us \\(\\frac{39}{40}\\), so 15.975 can be written as \\(15 \\frac{39}{40}\\) or \\(\\frac{639}{40}\\).\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buildings on the map is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\\) inches.", "To solve the problem, we need to determine the map distance that corresponds to a real-life distance of 53.25 miles, given that 3 inches on the map equates to 10 miles in real life. We can set up a proportion to solve this problem.\n\nThe proportion is:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{x \\text{ inches}}{53.25 \\text{ miles}}\n\\]\nwhere \\(x\\) is the map distance we are trying to find. We can solve for \\(x\\) by cross-multiplying and dividing:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches} \\times 53.25 \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{3 \\times 53.25}{10} \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\nmap_distance_per_10_miles = 3  # inches\nreal_distance = 53.25  # miles\nreal_distance_per_10_miles = 10  # miles\n\n# Calculate the map distance for 53.25 miles\nmap_distance = (map_distance_per_10_miles * real_distance) / real_distance_per_10_miles\nprint(map_distance)\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe map distance corresponding to a real-life distance of 53.25 miles is \\(15.975\\) inches. To express this as a fraction, we recognize that \\(15.975\\) can be written as \\(\\frac{15975}{1000}\\). We can simplify this fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15975 and 1000.\n\nLet's simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{15975}{1000}\\) using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Numerator and denominator\nnumerator = 15975\ndenominator = 1000\n\n# Find the greatest common divisor (GCD)\ngcd = math.gcd(numerator, denominator)\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nsimplified_numerator = numerator // gcd\nsimplified_denominator = denominator // gcd\n\nsimplified_fraction = (simplified_numerator, simplified_denominator)\nprint(simplified_fraction)\n```\n```output\n(639, 40)\n```\nThe simplified fraction for \\(15.975\\) inches is \\(\\frac{639}{40}\\).\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buildings on the map is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\\) inches.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the scale of the map and then use that scale to find the distance on the map that corresponds to a real-life distance of 53.25 miles.\n\nThe scale of the map is given as 3 inches on the map equates to 10 miles in real life. This means that for every 10 miles in real life, the distance on the map is 3 inches. We can express this scale as a ratio:\n\n\\[ \\text{Scale} = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nTo find the distance on the map for a real-life distance of 53.25 miles, we can set up the following proportion:\n\n\\[ \\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{x \\text{ inches}}{53.25 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches} \\times 53.25 \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\nscale_map_inches = 3  # inches on the map\nscale_real_miles = 10  # miles in real life\nreal_distance_miles = 53.25  # real-life distance in miles\n\n# Calculate the distance on the map\nmap_distance_inches = (scale_map_inches * real_distance_miles) / scale_real_miles\nprint(map_distance_inches)\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe distance between the buildings on the map is \\(15.975\\) inches. To express this as a fraction, we recognize that \\(15.975\\) is the same as \\(15 + \\frac{975}{1000}\\). Simplifying \\(\\frac{975}{1000}\\) gives us \\(\\frac{39}{40}\\), so the distance on the map is \\(15 \\frac{39}{40}\\) inches, or \\(\\frac{639}{40}\\) inches when expressed as an improper fraction.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to find out how many inches on the map correspond to 53.25 miles in real life, given that 3 inches on the map correspond to 10 miles in real life. We can set up a proportion to solve this:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{x \\text{ inches}}{53.25 \\text{ miles}}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches} \\times 53.25 \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ miles}}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\ninches_per_10_miles = 3\nreal_life_distance_miles = 53.25\nmiles_per_3_inches = 10\n\n# Calculate the distance on the map\ndistance_on_map_inches = (inches_per_10_miles * real_life_distance_miles) / miles_per_3_inches\nprint(distance_on_map_inches)\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe distance between the buildings on the map is \\(15.975\\) inches. To express this as a fraction, we note that \\(15.975\\) is the same as \\(15 \\frac{39}{40}\\) when converted to a mixed fraction. However, to express it purely as a fraction, we recognize that \\(15.975 = \\frac{15975}{1000} = \\frac{639}{40}\\).\n\nSo, the distance between the buildings on the map is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\\) inches.", "To solve the problem, we need to determine the distance on the map that corresponds to a real-life distance of 53.25 miles. Given that 3 inches on the map correspond to 10 miles in real life, we can set up a proportion to find the distance on the map.\n\nThe proportion can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{x \\text{ inches}}{53.25 \\text{ miles}}\n\\]\nwhere \\(x\\) is the distance on the map in inches. Solving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches} \\times 53.25 \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ miles}}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\nmap_distance_per_10_miles = 3  # inches\nreal_distance = 53.25  # miles\nreal_distance_per_10_miles = 10  # miles\n\n# Calculate the map distance for the given real distance\nmap_distance = (map_distance_per_10_miles * real_distance) / real_distance_per_10_miles\nprint(map_distance)\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe distance between the buildings on the map is \\(15.975\\) inches. To express this as a fraction, we recognize that \\(15.975\\) can be written as \\(15 \\frac{39}{40}\\) or \\(\\frac{639}{40}\\).\n\nSo, the distance between the buildings on the map is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\\) inches.", "Let's denote the original two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. The sum of the digits is given by \\(a + b = 13\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a\\). The difference between the original number and the reversed number is given by \\((10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\\). Simplifying this, we get \\(9a - 9b = 27\\), which further simplifies to \\(a - b = 3\\).\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\(a - b = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously. Adding the two equations, we get \\(2a = 16\\), so \\(a = 8\\). Substituting \\(a = 8\\) into the first equation, we get \\(8 + b = 13\\), so \\(b = 5\\). Therefore, the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\). The sum of the original number and the reversed number is \\(85 + 58 = 143\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the equations\r\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na, b = symbols('a b')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations based on the problem statement\r\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\r\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\r\na_val = solution[a]\r\nb_val = solution[b]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\r\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\r\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\r\n\r\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\r\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\r\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to set up a system of equations based on the information given. Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit. The number with its digits reversed would be \\(10b + a\\).\n\nWe are given two pieces of information:\n1. The sum of the digits is 13. This gives us the equation \\(a + b = 13\\).\n2. The difference between the number and the number with its digits reversed is 27. This gives us the equation \\((10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\\), which simplifies to \\(9a - 9b = 27\\) or \\(a - b = 3\\).\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + b = 13 \\\\\na - b = 3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[\n(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\implies 2a = 16 \\implies a = 8\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[\n8 + b = 13 \\implies b = 5\n\\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\), and the number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\). The sum of these two numbers is \\(85 + 58 = 143\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the equations\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. Here, \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit. The number with its digits reversed would be \\(10b + a\\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n1. The sum of the digits is 13. So, \\(a + b = 13\\).\n2. The difference between the original number and the reversed number is 27. So, \\(|10a + b - (10b + a)| = 27\\), which simplifies to \\(|9a - 9b| = 27\\), or \\(9|a - b| = 27\\). Therefore, \\(|a - b| = 3\\).\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\(|a - b| = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve these equations by considering the two possible cases for the absolute value equation: \\(a - b = 3\\) and \\(a - b = -3\\).\n\n### Case 1: \\(a - b = 3\\)\nAdding the equations \\(a + b = 13\\) and \\(a - b = 3\\):\n\\[ (a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\]\n\\[ 2a = 16 \\]\n\\[ a = 8 \\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) into \\(a + b = 13\\):\n\\[ 8 + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ b = 5 \\]\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\).\n\n### Case 2: \\(a - b = -3\\)\nAdding the equations \\(a + b = 13\\) and \\(a - b = -3\\):\n\\[ (a + b) + (a - b) = 13 - 3 \\]\n\\[ 2a = 10 \\]\n\\[ a = 5 \\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 5\\) into \\(a + b = 13\\):\n\\[ 5 + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ b = 8 \\]\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\).\n\nIn both cases, the sum of the original number and the reversed number is the same:\n\\[ 85 + 58 = 143 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's break down the problem into manageable parts and solve it step-by-step using Python and SymPy.\n\n1. **Define the Variables:**\n   Let the original two-digit number be \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. Here, \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit.\n\n2. **Formulate the Equations:**\n   - The sum of the digits is 13: \\(a + b = 13\\).\n   - The difference between the original number and the number with its digits reversed is 27: \\(10a + b - (10b + a) = 27\\). This simplifies to \\(9a - 9b = 27\\), or \\(a - b = 3\\).\n\n3. **Solve the System of Equations:**\n   We have two equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   a + b = 13 \\\\\n   a - b = 3\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   We can solve these equations to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\n4. **Find the Original Number and the Reversed Number:**\n   Once we have \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we can find the original number \\(10a + b\\) and the reversed number \\(10b + a\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the Sum:**\n   The sum of the original number and the reversed number is \\((10a + b) + (10b + a) = 11a + 11b = 11(a + b)\\). Since we know \\(a + b = 13\\), the sum is \\(11 \\times 13 = 143\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using SymPy to solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), and then calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = sp.symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = sp.Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\n\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number, with \\(a\\) being the tens digit and \\(b\\) being the units digit. The number with its digits reversed would then be \\(10b + a\\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the digits of the number is 13:\n\\[ a + b = 13 \\]\n\n2. The difference between the original number and the number with its digits reversed is 27:\n\\[ (10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27 \\]\n\\[ 10a + b - 10b - a = 27 \\]\n\\[ 9a - 9b = 27 \\]\n\\[ a - b = 3 \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[ a + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ a - b = 3 \\]\n\nWe can solve these equations by adding them together to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[ (a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\]\n\\[ 2a = 16 \\]\n\\[ a = 8 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ 8 + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ b = 5 \\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\), and the number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is:\n\\[ 85 + 58 = 143 \\]\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the equations\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]