 = solution[b]\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit. The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a\\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the digits is 13:\n\\[a + b = 13\\]\n\n2. The difference between the original number and the reversed number is 27:\n\\[10a + b - (10b + a) = 27\\]\n\\[10a + b - 10b - a = 27\\]\n\\[9a - 9b = 27\\]\n\\[a - b = 3\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[a + b = 13\\]\n\\[a - b = 3\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations:\n\\[(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3\\]\n\\[2a = 16\\]\n\\[a = 8\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) into the first equation:\n\\[8 + b = 13\\]\n\\[b = 5\\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the reversed number is:\n\\[85 + 58 = 143\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the equations and solve for a and b\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_