} 60 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 90 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 120\\] \\[\\boxed{\\text{Solution}}\\] Since $AB$ is a diameter, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle, so $\\angle AOC + \\angle COB = 180^\\circ$.  Since $\\angle AOB = 120^\\circ$, $\\angle COB = 180^\\circ - 120^\\circ = 60^\\circ$.  Therefore, $\\angle AOC = 180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = \\boxed{120}$.\n 10000 user\nA rectangle has a perimeter of 60 units and an area of 144 square units.  What is the length of the rectangle\'s shorter side? \\[\\textbf{(A) } 6 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 8 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 12 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 16 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 24\\] \\[\\boxed{\\text{Solution}}\\] Let the length of the shorter side be $x$ and the length of the longer side be $y$.  Then we have the equations \\begin{align*} 2x + 2y &= 60, \\\\ xy &= 144. \\end{align*}  Solving the first equation for $y$, we get $y = 30 - x$.  Substituting this into the second equation, we get $x(30 - x) = 144$, or $x^2 - 30x + 144 = 0$.  Factoring, we get $(x - 12)(x - 18) = 0$, so $x = 12$ or $x = 18$.  Since $x$ is the length of the shorter side, we have $x = \\boxed{12}$.\n 10001 user\nIn the diagram below, $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, and $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{CD}$.  What is the area of $\\triangle ABE$? \\[\\begin{asy} unitsize(1 cm);  pair A, B, C, D, E;  A = (0,0); B = (4,0); C = (4,4); D = (0,4); E = (D + C)/2;  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(A--E);  label("$A$", A, SW); label("$B$", B, SE); label("$C$", C, NE); label("$D$", D, NW); label("$E$", E, N); \\end{asy}\\] \\[\\textbf{(A) } 4 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 5 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 6 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 8 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 10\\] \\[\\boxed{\\text{Solution}}\\] Since $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, the area of $ABCD$ is $4^2 = 16$.  Since $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{CD}$, $\\triangle ABE$ is half of $ABCD$, so the area of $\\triangle ABE$ is $16/2 = \\boxed{8}$.\n 10002 user\nIn the diagram below, $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, and $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{CD}$.  What is the perimeter of $\\triangle ABE$? \\[\\begin{asy} unitsize(1 cm);  pair A, B, C, D, E;  A = (0,0); B = (4,0); C = (4,4); D = (0,4); E = (D + C)/2;  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(A--E);  label("$A$", A, SW); label("$B$", B, SE); label("$C$", C, NE); label("$D$", D, NW); label("$E$", E, N); \\end{asy}\\] \\[\\textbf{(A) } 8 + 2\\sqrt{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 8 + 4\\sqrt{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 12 + 2\\sqrt{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 12 + 4\\sqrt{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 16 + 2\\sqrt{2}\\] \\[\\boxed{\\text{Solution}}\\] Since $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, the perimeter of $ABCD$ is $4 \\cdot 4 = 16$.  Since $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{CD}$, $\\triangle ABE$ is half of $ABCD$, so the perimeter of $\\triangle ABE$ is $16/2 = \\boxed{8 + 2\\sqrt{2}}$.\n 10003 user\nIn the diagram below, $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, and $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{CD}$.  What is the length of $\\overline{AE}$? \\[\\begin{asy} unitsize(1 cm);  pair A, B, C, D, E;  A = (0,0); B = (4,0); C = (4,4); D = (0,4); E = (D + C)/2;  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(A--E);  label("$A$", A, SW); label("$B$", B, SE); label("$C$", C, NE); label("$D$", D, NW); label("$E$", E, N); \\end{asy}\\] \\[\\textbf{(A) } 2 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 2\\sqrt{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 3 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 4 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 5\\] \\[\\boxed{\\text{Solution}}\\] Since $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, $\\overline{AE}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of length 2 and 2.  By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of $\\overline{AE}$ is $\\sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{8} = \\boxed{2\\sqrt{2}}$.\n 10004 user\nIn the diagram below, $ABCD$ is a square with side length 4, and $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{CD}$.  What is the length of $\\overline{BE}$? \\[\\begin{asy} unitsize(1 cm);  pair A, B, C, D, E;  A = (0,0); B = (4,0); C = (4,4); D = (0,4); E = (D + C)/2;  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(A--E);  label("$A$", 