nt(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n40\n```\nThe measure of \\(\\angle STR\\) is \\(\\boxed{40}\\) degrees.', "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of parallel lines and angles. Here are the steps to find the measure of \\(\\angle STR\\):\n\n1. Since \\(\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}\\), the corresponding angles formed by the transversal \\(\\overline{PS}\\) will be equal. So, \\(\\angle P = \\angle PST = 40^\\circ\\).\n\n2. We know that the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, in \\(\\triangle PQS\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PQS + \\angle Q + \\angle PSQ = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   40^\\circ + 35^\\circ + \\angle PSQ = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(\\angle PSQ\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle PSQ = 180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 105^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\angle PSQ\\) and \\(\\angle STR\\) are corresponding angles (because \\(\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}\\)), \\(\\angle STR = \\angle PSQ = 105^\\circ\\).\n\nSo, the measure of \\(\\angle STR\\) is \\(105^\\circ\\). Let's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Calculate the third angle in triangle PQS\nangle_PQS = 180 - angle_P - angle_Q\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle PSQ (corresponding angles)\nangle_STR = angle_PQS\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n105\n```\nThe measure of \\(\\angle STR\\) is \\(\\boxed{105}\\) degrees.", "Given the diagram with $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, $\\angle P = 40^\\circ$, and $\\angle Q = 35^\\circ$, we need to find the measure of $\\angle STR$.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the problem. Since $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, the angles $\\angle P$ and $\\angle STR$ are corresponding angles. This means that $\\angle STR = \\angle P = 40^\\circ$.\n\nHowever, to confirm this, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$. In 