{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, the corresponding angles and alternate interior angles are equal. We can use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$.\n\nConsider the triangle $PQR$. The sum of the angles in triangle $PQR$ is:\n\\[\n\\angle P + \\angle Q + \\angle PRQ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nGiven $\\angle P = 40^\\circ$ and $\\angle Q = 35^\\circ$, we can find $\\angle PRQ$:\n\\[\n40^\\circ + 35^\\circ + \\angle PRQ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle PRQ = 180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 105^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, $\\angle STR$ is equal to $\\angle PRQ$ because they are corresponding angles. Therefore, $\\angle STR = 105^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Calculate angle PRQ\nangle_PRQ = 180 - angle_P - angle_Q\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle PRQ\nangle_STR = angle_PRQ\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n105\n```\nThe measure of $\\angle STR$ is \\(\\boxed{105}\\) degrees.", "To solve the problem, we need to evaluate the function \\(f(n)\\) for three different values: \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\), \\(\\sqrt{45}\\), and \\(8^{2/3}\\). We will then sum the results.\n\n1. Evaluate \\(f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\):\n   - Since \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.047\\), which is less than 4, we use the ceiling function \\(\\lceil n \\rceil\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\lceil 1.047 \\rceil = 2\\).\n\n2. Evaluate \\(f(\\sqrt{45})\\):\n   - Since \\(\\sqrt{45} \\approx 6.708\\), which is greater than or equal to 4, we use the floor function \\(\\lfloor n \\rfloor\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(f(\\sqrt{45}) = \\lfloor 6.708 \\rfloor = 6\\).\n\n3. Evaluate \\(f(8^{2/3})\\):\n   - Since \\(8^{2/3} = (2^3)^{2/3} = 2^2 = 4\\), which is greater than or equal to 4, we use the floor function \\(\\lfloor n \\rfloor\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(f(8^{2/3}) = \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4\\).\n\nFinally, we sum the results:\n\\[f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3}) = 2 + 6 + 4 = 12.\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the function f(n)\ndef f(n):\n    if n >= 4:\n        return math.floor(n)\n    else:\n        return math.ceil(n)\n\n# Values to evaluate\npi_over_3 = math.pi / 3\nsqrt_45 = math.sqrt(45)\neight_two_thirds = 8 ** (2 / 3)\n\n# Evaluate the function for each value\nresult = f(pi_over_3) + f(sqrt_45) + f(eight_two_thirds)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to evaluate the function \\( f \\) at three different points: \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\), \\(\\sqrt{45}\\), and \\(8^{2/3}\\). We will then sum the results.\n\nThe function \\( f \\) is defined as:\n\\[ f(n) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n\\lfloor n \\rfloor & \\text{ if } n \\geq 4, \\\\\n\\lceil n \\rceil & \\text{ if } n < 4.\n\\end{array}\n\\right. \\]\n\nLet's evaluate each term separately:\n\n1. For \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\):\n   - Since \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\), \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.047197\\).\n   - Since \\(1.047197 < 4\\), \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\lceil 1.047197 \\rceil = 2 \\).\n\n2. For \\( \\sqrt{45} \\):\n   - Since \\(45\\) is between \\(36\\) and \\(49\\), \\(\\sqrt{45}\\) is between \\(6\\) and \\(7\\). More precisely, \\(\\sqrt{45} \\approx 6.7082\\).\n   - Since \\(6.7082 \\geq 4\\), \\( f(\\sqrt{45}) = \\lfloor 6.7082 \\rfloor = 6 \\).\n\n3. For \\( 8^{2/3} \\):\n   - Since \\(8^{2/3} = (2^3)^{2/3} = 2^2 = 4\\), \\( f(8^{2/3}) = \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4 \\).\n\nNow we sum the results:\n\\[ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3}) = 2 + 6 + 4 = 12 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the function f\ndef f(n):\n    if n >= 4:\n        return math.floor(n)\n    else:\n        return math.ceil(n)\n\n# Evaluate f at the given points\npi_