ossible_value)\n```\n```output\n22\n```\nIt seems that the code is consistently outputting 22, which suggests that there might be another set of factors we are not considering. Let's manually verify the possible sets of distinct integers whose product is 33 and whose sum is minimized in absolute value.\n\nThe factorizations of 33 into distinct integers are:\n\\[33 = 33 \\times 1 = 33 \\times (-1) \\times (-1) = 11 \\times 3 = 11 \\times (-3) \\times (-1) = 11 \\times 3 \\times 1 = 11 \\times (-3) \\times 1 \\times (-1).\\]\nThe possible sets of distinct integers whose product is 33 are \\(\\{33, 1\\}\\), \\(\\{11, 3\\}\\), \\(\\{11, -3, -1\\}\\), and \\(\\{11, -3, 1, -1\\}\\). We need to check the sums of these sets:\n\\[33 + 1 = 34,\\]\n\\[11 + 3 = 14,\\]\n\\[11 - 3 - 1 = 7,\\]\n\\[11 - 3 + 1 - 1 = 8.\\]\nThe smallest absolute value of the sum is 7, which corresponds to the set \\(\\{11, -3, -1\\}\\). Therefore, the least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is \\(2 \\times 7 = 14\\).\n\nGiven the previous reasoning, the correct answer should be 14. Let's confirm this with a corrected Python code approach. We will manually check the possible sets of factors and their sums.\n```python\n# Define the product of the roots\nproduct_of_roots = 33\n\n# Define a function to find the minimum absolute sum of distinct factors of the product\ndef find_min_abs_sum(product):\n    min_sum = float('inf')\n    min_abs_sum = float('inf')\n    # Iterate over possible factors\n    for i in range(1, int(product**0.5) + 1):\n        if product % i == 0:\n            # Check positive and negative pairs\n            factors = [i, product // i]\n            if len(factors) == 2:\n                current_sum = sum(factors)\n                if abs(current_sum) < min_abs_sum:\n                    min_abs_sum = abs(current_sum)\n                    min_sum = current_sum\n            # Check if we can add more factors\n            for j in range(-i, i + 1):\n                if j != 0 and product %