cdot (-3) \\cdot 11 \\), which gives the roots \\( r_1 = -1, r_2 = -3, r_3 = 11 \\). The sum of these roots is \\( -1 - 3 + 11 = 7 \\) (or \\( -7 \\) if we take the negatives). Therefore, \\( a_{n-1} = -2 \\cdot 7 = -14 \\) (or \\( 14 \\)).\n\nThis is the smallest possible value we can get. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the least possible value of |a_{n-1}|\ndef find_min_abs_a_n_minus_1(a_n, a_0):\n    # Factors of a_0 / a_n\n    factors = []\n    for i in range(1, abs(a_0 // a_n) + 1):\n        if (a_0 // a_n) % i == 0:\n            factors.append(i)\n            factors.append(-i)\n    \n    min_abs_a_n_minus_1 = float('inf')\n    \n    # Check all combinations of factors\n    from itertools import combinations\n    for r in combinations(factors, 3):\n        if r[0] * r[1] * r[2] == a_0 // a_n:\n            sum_roots = sum(r)\n            abs_a_n_minus_1 = abs(2 * sum_roots)\n            if abs_a_n_minus_1 < min_abs_a_n_minus_1:\n                min_abs_a_n_minus_1 = abs_a_n_minus_1\n    \n    return min_abs_a_n_minus_1\n\n# Given values\na_n = 2\na_0 = 66\n\n# Find the least possible value of |a_{n-1}|\nmin_abs_a_n_minus_1 = find_min_abs_a_n_minus_1(a_n, a_0)\nprint(min_abs_a_n_minus_1)\n```\n```output\n14\n```\nThe least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) for the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).", "Given the polynomial \\(f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66\\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots, we need to find the least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\).\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are integers, and since the leading coefficient \\(a_n = 2\\), the polynomial can be written as:\n\\[f(x) = 2(x - r_1)(x - r_2)\\cdots(x - r_n)\\]\nwhere \\(r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n\\) are the distinct integer roots.\n\nThe constant term \\(a_0 = 66\\) is the product of the roots (with the sign adjusted for the leading coefficient):\n\\[2(-r_1)(-r_2)\\cdots(-r_n) = 66\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[2(-1)^n r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = 66\\]\nor\n\\[r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = \\frac{66}{2(-1)^n} = 33(-1)^{n+1}\\]\nThis means the product of the roots (with the appropriate sign) is 33. The integer factorizations of 33 are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 11, \\pm 33\\).\n\nTo minimize \\(|a_{n-1}|\\), we need to consider the sum of the roots (with the appropriate signs) because \\(a_{n-1} = 2(-1)^{n+1}(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n)\\).\n\nLet's consider the possible factorizations of 33 and calculate \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) for each case:\n\n1. If \\(n = 2\\), the roots could be \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 11, \\pm 33\\). The possible pairs are \\((1, 33)\\), \\((-1, -33)\\), \\((3, 11)\\), \\((-3, -11)\\). The sums are \\(34, -34, 14, -14\\), so \\(|a_{n-1}| = 2 \\cdot 14 = 28\\).\n2. If \\(n = 3\\), the roots could be \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 11\\). The possible triplets are \\((1, 3, 11)\\), \\((-1, -3, -11)\\), \\((1, -3, -11)\\), \\((-1, 3, 11)\\). The sums are \\(15, -15, -13, 13\\), so \\(|a_{n-1}| = 2 \\cdot 13 = 26\\).\n3. If \\(n = 4\\), the roots could be \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 11\\). The possible quadruplets are \\((1, 1, 3, 11)\\), \\((-1, -1, -3, -11)\\), \\((1, -1, 3, -11)\\), \\((-1, 1, -3, 11)\\). The sums are \\(15, -15, -13, 13\\), so \\(|a_{n-1}| = 2 \\cdot 13 = 26\\).\n\nFrom the above calculations, the least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is 26. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations_with_replacement, permutations\n\n# Function to find the least possible value of |a_{n-1}|\ndef find_min_abs_a_n_minus_1():\n    # Possible factors of 33\n    factors = [1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 33, -33]\n    \n    min_abs_a_n_minus_1 = float('inf')\n    \n    # Check for n = 2\n    for r1, r2 in combinations_with_replacement(factors, 2):\n        if r1 * r2 == 33 or r1 * r2 == -33:\n            a_n_minu