ength_1 = 1\nside_length_2 = sp.sqrt(2) / 2\n\n# Calculate the side length of the central square\ncentral_square_side = side_length_1 - side_length_2\n\n# Calculate the area of the central square\ncentral_square_area = central_square_side**2\n\n# Calculate the area of one right-angled triangle\ntriangle_area = (1/2) * (side_length_2) * (side_length_2)\n\n# Calculate the total area of the four triangles\ntotal_triangle_area = 4 * triangle_area\n\n# Calculate the total area of the octagon\ntotal_area = central_square_area + total_triangle_area\n\n# Simplify the result\ntotal_area_simplified = sp.simplify(total_area)\nprint(total_area_simplified)\n```\n```output\n2.0 - 0.5*sqrt(2)\n```\nThe output from the code is \\(2.0 - 0.5\\sqrt{2}\\). This is the area of the equiangular octagon with sides of length 1 and \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n\nSo, the area of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{2 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\).", "Let's break down the problem step by step. An equiangular octagon means all internal angles are equal to \\(135^\\circ\\). The side lengths alternate between 1 and \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). We can visualize the octagon as a square with four right-angled triangles removed from its corners.\n\n1. Start by considering a square with side length \\(a\\). The area of the square is \\(a^2\\).\n2. Four right-angled triangles are removed from the corners of the square. Each of these triangles has legs of length \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\) and \\(1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). The area of each triangle is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})